body:
{
  "body": {
    "desktop": [{
      "type": "asset",
      "value": "74102192007"
    }, {
      "type": "text",
      "value": "\u003cp\u003eNick Mavar Jr., a deckhand for the F/V Northwestern who was featured on the series \u0026#34;Deadliest Catch\u0026#34; over the course of 15 years, has died.\u003c/p\u003e"
    }, {
      "type": "text",
      "value": "\u003cp\u003eHe was 59 years old.\u003c/p\u003e"
    }, {
      "type": "text",
      "value": "\u003cp\u003eMavar, whose real name Nickola Mavar Jr., died Thursday in Naknek, Alaska, following a medical emergency, Bristol Bay Borough Police Chief Jeff Elbie told USA TODAY Friday. The longtime fisherman was pronounced dead after he was transported to a medical facility. \u003c/p\u003e"
    }, {
      "type": "text",
      "value": "\u003cp\u003eUSA TODAY has reached out to representatives for the F/V Northwestern and Discovery for comment. \u003c/p\u003e"
    }, {
      "type": "text",
      "value": "\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href\u003d\"https://www.tmz.com/2024/06/14/deadliest-catch-star-nick-mavar-dead-dies-medical-emergency/\" target\u003d\"_blank\" rel\u003d\"noreferrer noopener\"\u003eTMZ\u003c/a\u003e was first to report the news.\u003c/p\u003e"
    }, {
      "type": "text",
      "value": "\u003cp\u003eMavar appeared on 16 seasons of \u0026#34;Deadliest Catch,\u0026#34; which started airing on Discovery Channel in 2005. Fifteen years after making his debut in Season 2, Mavar\u0026#39;s final appearance on the Emmy-nominated show was in Season 17, which aired in 2021.\u003c/p\u003e"
    }, {
      "type": "text",
      "value": "\u003cp\u003e\u0026#34;Deadliest Catch,\u0026#34; now in its 20th season, follows crab fisherman off the coast of Alaska as they continuously defy death during expeditions in the Bering Sea. \u003c/p\u003e"
    }, {
      "type": "asset",
      "value": "72087830007"
    }, {
      "type": "text",
