  }, {
    "type": "text",
    "value": "\u003cp\u003eI also reminisced with the guys in an interview \u003ca href\u003d\"https://www.usatoday.com/story/entertainment/movies/2024/06/06/bad-boys-4-will-smith-martin-lawrence-reminisce/73953775007/\" target\u003d\"_blank\" rel\u003d\"noreferrer noopener\"\u003elooking back at the franchise\u003c/a\u003e, including Smith talking about their first meeting 30 years ago: \u0026#34;I felt like Joe Frazier with Ali.”\u003c/p\u003e"
  }, {
    "type": "text",
    "value": "\u003cp\u003e\u003cspan class\u003d\"exclude-from-newsgate\"\u003e\u003cstrong style\u003d\"margin-right:3px;\"\u003eJoin our Watch Party! \u003c/strong\u003e\u003ca href\u003d\"https://profile.usatoday.com/newsletters/watch-party/\" target\u003d\"_blank\"\u003eSign up to receive USA TODAY\u0026#39;s movie and TV recommendations right in your inbox\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/span\u003e\u003c/p\u003e"
  }, {
    "type": "asset",
    "value": "73427001007"
  }, {
    "type": "text",
    "value": "\u003ch2 class\u003d\"presto-h2 wp-block-heading\"\u003eCheck out Glen Powell in Netflix\u0026#39;s \u0026#39;Hit Man\u0026#39; (one of the best movies of 2024!)\u003c/h2\u003e"
  }, {
    "type": "asset",
    "value": "73984623007"
  }, {
    "type": "text",
    "value": "\u003cp\u003eGlen Powell has had quite the rise in recent years, flying high as the \u003ca href\u003d\"https://www.usatoday.com/story/entertainment/movies/2022/05/26/top-gun-maverick-hangman-pilot-glen-powell/9847096002/?utm_source\u003dfeedblitz\u0026utm_medium\u003dFeedBlitzRss\u0026utm_campaign\u003dusatodaycommovies-topstories\" target\u003d\"_blank\" rel\u003d\"noreferrer noopener\"\u003ecoolest \u0026#34;Top Gun\u0026#34; pilot\u003c/a\u003e and \u003ca href\u003d\"https://www.usatoday.com/story/entertainment/movies/2023/12/20/sydney-sweeney-glen-powell-anyone-but-you-interview/71958216007/\" target\u003d\"_blank\" rel\u003d\"noreferrer noopener\"\u003esinging Natasha Bedingfield hits naked\u003c/a\u003e. His latest movie is \u003ca href\u003d\"https://www.usatoday.com/story/entertainment/movies/2024/06/05/2024-best-new-movies/73875335007/\" target\u003d\"_blank\" rel\u003d\"noreferrer noopener\"\u003eone of this year\u0026#39;s best so far\u003c/a\u003e: Powell co-wrote and stars in \u0026#34;Hit Man\u0026#34; (\u003ca href\u003d\"https://www.netflix.com/title/81728840\" target\u003d\"_blank\" rel\u003d\"noreferrer noopener\"\u003estreaming on Netflix\u003c/a\u003e) as a fake contract killer who works undercover for the cops. Things get dicey when he falls for a woman (Adria Arjona) who wants to off her husband.\u003c/p\u003e"
  }, {
    "type": "text",
    "value": "\u003cp\u003eWhen it comes to Hollywood dream roles, \u0026#34;you have to bake it up yourself,” Powell says in a \u003ca href\u003d\"https://www.usatoday.com/story/entertainment/movies/2024/06/07/hit-man-movie-2024-glen-powell-interview/73953847007/\" target\u003d\"_blank\" rel\u003d\"noreferrer noopener\"\u003ewide-ranging interview\u003c/a\u003e that also includes his adorable dog Brisket and new summer flick \u0026#34;Twisters.\u0026#34;\u003c/p\u003e"
  }, {
    "type": "asset",
    "value": "73185236007"
  }, {
    "type": "text",
    "value": "\u003ch2 class\u003d\"presto-h2 wp-block-heading\"\u003eStream the latest \u0026#39;Star Wars\u0026#39; series \u0026#39;The Acolyte\u0026#39; for a Jedi jam\u003c/h2\u003e"
  }, {
    "type": "asset",
    "value": "74001297007"
  }, {
    "type": "text",
    "value": "\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href\u003d\"https://www.usatoday.com/story/entertainment/movies/2023/05/24/star-wars-characters-definitively-ranked/70209447007/\" target\u003d\"_blank\" rel\u003d\"noreferrer noopener\"\u003e\u0026#34;Star Wars\u0026#34;\u003c/a\u003e confession time: I don\u0026#39;t \u003cem\u003eloooooove\u003c/em\u003e the Jedi. And lightsaber duels are fine, but I\u0026#39;d rather have an X-wing/TIE Fighter dogfight or a bombing run on a Death Star instead. So on paper, the new \u0026#34;Star Wars\u0026#34; series \u003ca href\u003d\"https://www.usatoday.com/story/entertainment/movies/2024/05/04/upcoming-star-wars-movies-tv-shows/73526499007/\" target\u003d\"_blank\" rel\u003d\"noreferrer noopener\"\u003e\u0026#34;The Acolyte\u0026#34;\u003c/a\u003e (first two episodes \u003ca href\u003d\"https://www.disneyplus.com/browse/entity-a5c84b7d-fd4e-42c8-95e3-960e73e1a71e\" target\u003d\"_blank\" rel\u003d\"noreferrer noopener\"\u003estreaming on Disney+\u003c/a\u003e) shouldn\u0026#39;t be my jam. But it\u0026#39;s got some things working in its favor, including a nifty murder-mystery angle (with the Jedi Order investigating a killing spree) and a kung fu influence.\u003c/p\u003e"
  }, {
    "type": "text",
    "value": "\u003cp\u003eBut our TV critic Kelly Lawler was less enthused, saying the show boasts the worst elements of the prequel movie trilogy: \u0026#34;Full of logical fallacies, hokey dialogue and nonsensical plots.\u0026#34; (Read her \u003ca href\u003d\"https://www.usatoday.com/story/entertainment/tv/2024/06/04/the-acolyte-star-wars-review/73876874007/\" target\u003d\"_blank\" rel\u003d\"noreferrer noopener\"\u003e★★ review here\u003c/a\u003e.)\u003c/p\u003e"
  }, {
    "type": "text",
    "value": "\u003ch2 class\u003d\"presto-h2 wp-block-heading\"\u003eEven more goodness to check out!\u003c/h2\u003e"
  }, {
    "type": "text",
    "value": "\u003cul\u003e\n\u003cli\u003eDirector \u003ca href\u003d\"https://www.usatoday.com/story/entertainment/movies/2024/06/06/the-watchers-ishana-night-shyamalan/73953836007/\" target\u003d\"_blank\" rel\u003d\"noreferrer noopener\"\u003eIshana Night Shyamalan\u003c/a\u003e discusses her debut horror fantasy \u0026#34;The Watchers.\u0026#34; (Sign me up for one of her famous dad\u0026#39;s movie nights.)\u003c/li\u003e\n\n\n\n\u003cli\u003eAre you emotionally prepared for the end of Pat Sajak\u0026#39;s epic \u003ca href\u003d\"https://www.usatoday.com/story/entertainment/tv/2024/06/05/pat-sajak-final-wheel-of-fortune-episode-finale/73960783007/\" target\u003d\"_blank\" rel\u003d\"noreferrer noopener\"\u003e\u0026#34;Wheel of Fortune\u0026#34;\u003c/a\u003e run?\u003c/li\u003e\n\n\n\n\u003cli\u003eIs it OK to come out in your 30s? Dakota Johnson\u0026#39;s new movie \u003ca href\u003d\"https://www.usatoday.com/story/entertainment/movies/2024/06/06/am-i-ok-2024-movie-streaming-max/73957941007/\" target\u003d\"_blank\" rel\u003d\"noreferrer noopener\"\u003e\u0026#34;Am I OK?\u0026#34;\u003c/a\u003e shows \u0026#34;there is no timeline.\u0026#34; (Fun fact: It\u0026#39;s better than \u0026#34;Madame Web\u0026#34;!)\u003c/li\u003e\n\u003c/ul\u003e"
  }, {
    "type": "text",
    "value": "\u003cp\u003eGot thoughts, questions, ideas, concerns, compliments or maybe even some recs for me? Email btruitt@usatoday.com and follow me on the socials: I\u0026#39;m @briantruitt on \u003ca href\u003d\"https://twitter.com/briantruitt\" target\u003d\"_blank\" rel\u003d\"noreferrer noopener\"\u003eTwitter\u003c/a\u003e (not calling it X!), \u003ca href\u003d\"https://www.instagram.com/briantruitt/\" target\u003d\"_blank\" rel\u003d\"noreferrer noopener\"\u003eInstagram\u003c/a\u003e and \u003ca href\u003d\"https://www.threads.net/@briantruitt\" target\u003d\"_blank\" rel\u003d\"noreferrer noopener\"\u003eThreads\u003c/a\u003e.\u003c/p\u003e"
  }, {
    "type": "text",
    "value": " "
  }]
}

body:
{
  "body": {
    "desktop": [{
      "type": "asset",
      "value": "74008865007"
    }, {
      "type": "text",
      "value": "\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003e\u003cem\u003eSpoiler alert! We\u0026#39;re discussing plot points in Netflix\u0026#39;s new fake assassin movie \u0026#34;Hit Man.\u0026#34; Stop reading now if you haven\u0026#39;t seen it yet and don\u0026#39;t want to know.\u003c/em\u003e\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/p\u003e"
    }, {
      "type": "text",
      "value": "\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href\u003d\"https://www.usatoday.com/story/entertainment/movies/2024/06/07/hit-man-movie-2024-glen-powell-interview/73953847007/\" data-type\u003d\"link\" data-id\u003d\"https://www.usatoday.com/story/entertainment/movies/2024/06/07/hit-man-movie-2024-glen-powell-interview/73953847007/\" target\u003d\"_blank\" rel\u003d\"noreferrer noopener\"\u003e\u0026#34;Hit Man\u0026#34;\u003c/a\u003e is a romantic comedy that aims to kill your notion of who a hit man can be.\u003c/p\u003e"
    }, {
      "type": "text",
      "value": "\u003cp\u003eThe Netflix movie stars \u003ca href\u003d\"https://www.usatoday.com/story/entertainment/celebrities/2024/05/16/glen-powell-parents-troll-hit-man-premiere/73717334007/\" data-type\u003d\"link\" data-id\u003d\"https://www.usatoday.com/story/entertainment/celebrities/2024/05/16/glen-powell-parents-troll-hit-man-premiere/73717334007/\" target\u003d\"_blank\" rel\u003d\"noreferrer noopener\"\u003eGlen Powell\u003c/a\u003e (\u0026#34;\u003ca href\u003d\"https://www.usatoday.com/story/entertainment/movies/2022/05/26/top-gun-maverick-hangman-pilot-glen-powell/9847096002/\" data-type\u003d\"link\" data-id\u003d\"https://www.usatoday.com/story/entertainment/movies/2022/05/26/top-gun-maverick-hangman-pilot-glen-powell/9847096002/\" target\u003d\"_blank\" rel\u003d\"noreferrer noopener\"\u003eTop Gun\u003c/a\u003e,\u0026#34; \u0026#34;\u003ca href\u003d\"https://www.usatoday.com/story/entertainment/celebrities/2024/04/23/anyone-but-you-glen-powell-sydney-sweeney-affair-rumors/73433726007/\" data-type\u003d\"link\" data-id\u003d\"https://www.usatoday.com/story/entertainment/celebrities/2024/04/23/anyone-but-you-glen-powell-sydney-sweeney-affair-rumors/73433726007/\" target\u003d\"_blank\" rel\u003d\"noreferrer noopener\"\u003eAnyone But You\u003c/a\u003e\u0026#34;) as Gary Johnson, a real-life undercover police officer who made a name for himself by posing as a fake assassin for hire. He would meet with people who wanted to murder a spouse, colleague, boss and so on, and once they uttered their desire for the hit and gave Johnson money, the recorded transaction often landed those individuals in jail.\u003c/p\u003e"
    }, {
      "type": "text",
      "value": "\u003cp\u003ePowell co-wrote \u0026#34;Hit Man,\u0026#34; which is based on a 2001 \u003ca href\u003d\"https://www.texasmonthly.com/true-crime/hit-man-2/\" target\u003d\"_blank\" rel\u003d\"noreferrer noopener\"\u003eTexas Monthly article by Skip Hollandsworth\u003c/a\u003e, with director Richard Linklater (\u003ca href\u003d\"https://www.usatoday.com/story/life/movies/2014/07/09/boyhood-richard-linklater-ethan-hawke-astros/12377511/\" data-type\u003d\"link\" data-id\u003d\"https://www.usatoday.com/story/life/movies/2014/07/09/boyhood-richard-linklater-ethan-hawke-astros/12377511/\" target\u003d\"_blank\" rel\u003d\"noreferrer noopener\"\u003e\u0026#34;Boyhood,\u0026#34;\u003c/a\u003e \u003ca href\u003d\"https://www.usatoday.com/story/life/movies/2016/03/31/richard-linklater-blake-jenner-everybody-wants-some/82425704/\" data-type\u003d\"link\" data-id\u003d\"https://www.usatoday.com/story/life/movies/2016/03/31/richard-linklater-blake-jenner-everybody-wants-some/82425704/\" target\u003d\"_blank\" rel\u003d\"noreferrer noopener\"\u003e\u0026#34;Everybody Wants Some!!\u0026#34;\u003c/a\u003e). Powell, 35, told USA TODAY that he dove into the script in order to grab a role he didn\u0026#39;t think he\u0026#39;d otherwise get. \u003c/p\u003e"
    }, {
      "type": "text",
      "value": "\u003cp\u003e\u003cspan class\u003d\"exclude-from-newsgate\"\u003e\u003cstrong style\u003d\"margin-right:3px;\"\u003eJoin our Watch Party!  \u003c/strong\u003e\u003ca href\u003d\"https://profile.usatoday.com/newsletters/watch-party/\" target\u003d\"_blank\"\u003eSign up to receive USA TODAY\u0026#39;s movie and TV recommendations right in your inbox\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/span\u003e\u003c/p\u003e"
    }, {
      "type": "asset",
      "value": "74040193007"
    }, {
      "type": "text",
      "value": "\u003cp\u003e\u0026#34;The one thing that you realize about Hollywood is no one\u0026#39;s ever going to give you the role that you\u0026#39;ve dreamed of,\u0026#34; he said. \u0026#34;You have to bake it up yourself.\u0026#34;  \u003c/p\u003e"
    }, {
      "type": "text",
      "value": "\u003cp\u003eLinklater says he was drawn to the project as a way explore the often cliché notion of a killer for hire.\u003c/p\u003e"
    }, {
      "type": "text",
      "value": "\u003cp\u003e\u0026#34;This is the weirdest job ever, being a fake hit man for people who want someone dead,\u0026#34; \u003ca href\u003d\"https://www.statesman.com/story/entertainment/movies/2024/06/06/richard-linklater-hit-man-glen-powell-indie-films-austin-film-society-failure-of-hollywood/73975095007/\" data-type\u003d\"link\" data-id\u003d\"https://www.statesman.com/story/entertainment/movies/2024/06/06/richard-linklater-hit-man-glen-powell-indie-films-austin-film-society-failure-of-hollywood/73975095007/\" target\u003d\"_blank\" rel\u003d\"noreferrer noopener\"\u003eLinklater told the Austin American-Statesman\u003c/a\u003e, part of the USA TODAY Network. \u0026#34;It was more about the myth of the hit man. And these things don’t exist; hit men are created by movies. It’s a pop culture delusion. So I’m kind of interested in that too, about people’s beliefs. I kind of enjoy deconstructing and debunking.\u0026#34;\u003c/p\u003e"
    }, {
      "type": "text",
      "value": "\u003cp\u003eHow much of \u0026#34;Hit Man\u0026#34; is bunk, and how much is based on the real Johnson\u0026#39;s exploits? We explore:\u003c/p\u003e"
    }, {
      "type": "asset",
      "value": "73427001007"
    }, {
      "type": "text",
      "value": "\u003ch2 class\u003d\"presto-h2 wp-block-heading\"\u003eIs Gary Johnson, the hero of Netflix\u0026#39;s \u0026#39;Hit Man,\u0026#39; a real person?\u003c/h2\u003e"
    }, {
      "type": "text",
      "value": "\u003cp\u003eAs depicted in the movie, Johnson was a mild-mannered professor who taught classes in human sexuality and general psychology at a community college. While the film is set in New Orleans, Johnson lived in Houston and worked undercover alongside local law enforcement. Wearing a wire, he would meet with people soliciting hit men and coax out confessions.\u003c/p\u003e"
    }, {
      "type": "text",
      "value": "\u003cp\u003eAccording to Texas Monthly, Johnson was considered \u0026#34;the Laurence Olivier of the field\u0026#34; by his peers. He assisted in the arrests of more than 70 people seeking contract killers: Using aliases such as Mike Caine and Jody Eagle, he posed as everything from country-club sophisticates to rough-and-tumble bikers.\u003c/p\u003e"
    }, {
      "type": "asset",
      "value": "73984623007"
    }, {
      "type": "text",
      "value": "\u003cp\u003e“He’s the perfect chameleon,” Houston lawyer Michael Hinton told Hollandsworth. “He never gets flustered, and he never says the wrong thing. ... He fools them every time.”\u003c/p\u003e"
    }, {
      "type": "text",
      "value": "\u003ch2 class\u003d\"presto-h2 wp-block-heading\"\u003eDid Gary Johnson really fall in love with a woman who wanted him to kill her husband?\u003c/h2\u003e"
    }, {
      "type": "text",
      "value": "\u003cp\u003eEarly in the film, Johnson is contacted by a young woman named Madison Masters (Adria Arjona), or Maddy, who asks him to kill her abusive husband, Ray (Evan Holtzman). Instead, Johnson tells her to keep her money, leave her spouse and start a new life. The two fall into bed together and start a relationship, which quickly turns dangerous when Ray shows up seeking vengeance.\u003c/p\u003e"
    }, {
      "type": "text",
      "value": "\u003cp\u003eAccording to Hollandsworth\u0026#39;s article, there is no evidence that Johnson dated one of his potential \u0026#34;clients.\u0026#34; He does detail instances in which women found Johnson seductive and tried to have sex with him.\u003c/p\u003e"
    }, {
      "type": "text",
      "value": "\u003cp\u003eIn the movie, Johnson is divorced; in reality, Johnson was divorced three times and, according to Texas Monthly, was mostly a soft-spoken loner who enjoyed his cats and reading books by famous philosophers.\u003c/p\u003e"
    }, {
      "type": "text",
      "value": "\u003ch2 class\u003d\"presto-h2 wp-block-heading\"\u003eDid Gary Johnson really talk a client out of ordering a hit?\u003c/h2\u003e"
    }, {
      "type": "text",
      "value": "\u003cp\u003eWhile defense attorneys would argue that Johnson steered his \u0026#34;clients\u0026#34; toward hiring him to commit murder, Johnson maintained that he was trying to help these individuals understand their true desires more clearly. \u003c/p\u003e"
    }, {
      "type": "asset",
      "value": "73185236007"
    }, {
      "type": "text",
      "value": "\u003cp\u003eNever was that more true when he met a young woman at a Starbucks who wanted her boyfriend dead. The “Hit Man” movie uses the unnamed woman\u0026#39;s story as a jumping-off point for the second half of the movie, but the romance was purely fictional.\u003c/p\u003e"
    }, {
      "type": "text",
      "value": "\u003cp\u003eJohnson \u0026#34;learned that she really was the victim of abuse, regularly battered by her boyfriend, too terrified to leave him because of her fear of what he might do if he found her,” Hollandsworth writes. “Instead of setting up a sting to catch the woman and send her off to jail, he decided to help her. He referred her to social service agencies and a therapist to make sure she got proper help so she could leave her boyfriend and get into a women’s shelter.”\u003c/p\u003e"
    }, {
      "type": "text",
      "value": "\u003cp\u003eBut in most cases, even when Johnson would repeatedly ask clients to confirm they wanted to hire a killer, most ultimately said yes, the article says.\u003c/p\u003e"
    }, {
      "type": "text",
      "value": "\u003ch2 class\u003d\"presto-h2 wp-block-heading\"\u003eDid the murders in the Netflix movie \u0026#39;Hit Man\u0026#39; really happen?\u003c/h2\u003e"
    }, {
      "type": "asset",
      "value": "73678332007"
    }, {
      "type": "text",
      "value": "\u003cp\u003eIn \u0026#34;Hit Man,\u0026#34; Powell\u0026#39;s Johnson has fallen in love with Maddy (Arjona) and is determined to ensure that the two have a life together, despite the duo being in serious legal jeopardy. In the movie, Madison has killed her abusive ex-husband, Ray (Holtzman), who tried to take out a hit on her. A police colleague of Johnson\u0026#39;s, Jasper (Austin Amelio), finds out and threatens to turn the couple in unless he is given the million-dollar payment from Ray\u0026#39;s insurance policy.\u003c/p\u003e"
    }, {
      "type": "text",
      "value": "\u003cp\u003eAs Jasper lays out his demands to Gary and Maddy, he suddenly slumps over, as the drugs Maddy has slipped into his drink take effect. While he isn\u0026#39;t quite dead, Gary makes the decision to put a plastic bag over Jasper\u0026#39;s head, killing him. The movie then cuts to a few years down the road, in which, murders be damned, Gary and Maddy have two children and appear to be living happily ever after.\u003c/p\u003e"
    }, {
      "type": "text",
      "value": "\u003cp\u003eThe real Gary Johnson never killed anyone. A title card at the end of movie says Johnson was an animal-loving Buddhist, a Vietnam War veteran and the “chilliest dude imaginable.” He was born in 1947 and died in 2022 before “Hit Man” started shooting, although his cause of death is unknown.  \u003c/p\u003e"
    }, {
      "type": "text",
      "value": "\u003cp\u003e\u003cem\u003eContributing: Brian Truitt\u003c/em\u003e\u003c/p\u003e"
    }],
    "mobile": [{
      "type": "asset",
      "value": "74008865007"
    }, {
      "type": "text",
      "value": "\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003e\u003cem\u003eSpoiler alert! We\u0026#39;re discussing plot points in Netflix\u0026#39;s new fake assassin movie \u0026#34;Hit Man.\u0026#34; Stop reading now if you haven\u0026#39;t seen it yet and don\u0026#39;t want to know.\u003c/em\u003e\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/p\u003e"
    }, {
      "type": "text",
      "value": "\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href\u003d\"https://www.usatoday.com/story/entertainment/movies/2024/06/07/hit-man-movie-2024-glen-powell-interview/73953847007/\" data-type\u003d\"link\" data-id\u003d\"https://www.usatoday.com/story/entertainment/movies/2024/06/07/hit-man-movie-2024-glen-powell-interview/73953847007/\" target\u003d\"_blank\" rel\u003d\"noreferrer noopener\"\u003e\u0026#34;Hit Man\u0026#34;\u003c/a\u003e is a romantic comedy that aims to kill your notion of who a hit man can be.\u003c/p\u003e"
    }, {
      "type": "text",
      "value": "\u003cp\u003eThe Netflix movie stars \u003ca href\u003d\"https://www.usatoday.com/story/entertainment/celebrities/2024/05/16/glen-powell-parents-troll-hit-man-premiere/73717334007/\" data-type\u003d\"link\" data-id\u003d\"https://www.usatoday.com/story/entertainment/celebrities/2024/05/16/glen-powell-parents-troll-hit-man-premiere/73717334007/\" target\u003d\"_blank\" rel\u003d\"noreferrer noopener\"\u003eGlen Powell\u003c/a\u003e (\u0026#34;\u003ca href\u003d\"https://www.usatoday.com/story/entertainment/movies/2022/05/26/top-gun-maverick-hangman-pilot-glen-powell/9847096002/\" data-type\u003d\"link\" data-id\u003d\"https://www.usatoday.com/story/entertainment/movies/2022/05/26/top-gun-maverick-hangman-pilot-glen-powell/9847096002/\" target\u003d\"_blank\" rel\u003d\"noreferrer noopener\"\u003eTop Gun\u003c/a\u003e,\u0026#34; \u0026#34;\u003ca href\u003d\"https://www.usatoday.com/story/entertainment/celebrities/2024/04/23/anyone-but-you-glen-powell-sydney-sweeney-affair-rumors/73433726007/\" data-type\u003d\"link\" data-id\u003d\"https://www.usatoday.com/story/entertainment/celebrities/2024/04/23/anyone-but-you-glen-powell-sydney-sweeney-affair-rumors/73433726007/\" target\u003d\"_blank\" rel\u003d\"noreferrer noopener\"\u003eAnyone But You\u003c/a\u003e\u0026#34;) as Gary Johnson, a real-life undercover police officer who made a name for himself by posing as a fake assassin for hire. He would meet with people who wanted to murder a spouse, colleague, boss and so on, and once they uttered their desire for the hit and gave Johnson money, the recorded transaction often landed those individuals in jail.\u003c/p\u003e"
    }, {
      "type": "text",
      "value": "\u003cp\u003ePowell co-wrote \u0026#34;Hit Man,\u0026#34; which is based on a 2001 \u003ca href\u003d\"https://www.texasmonthly.com/true-crime/hit-man-2/\" target\u003d\"_blank\" rel\u003d\"noreferrer noopener\"\u003eTexas Monthly article by Skip Hollandsworth\u003c/a\u003e, with director Richard Linklater (\u003ca href\u003d\"https://www.usatoday.com/story/life/movies/2014/07/09/boyhood-richard-linklater-ethan-hawke-astros/12377511/\" data-type\u003d\"link\" data-id\u003d\"https://www.usatoday.com/story/life/movies/2014/07/09/boyhood-richard-linklater-ethan-hawke-astros/12377511/\" target\u003d\"_blank\" rel\u003d\"noreferrer noopener\"\u003e\u0026#34;Boyhood,\u0026#34;\u003c/a\u003e \u003ca href\u003d\"https://www.usatoday.com/story/life/movies/2016/03/31/richard-linklater-blake-jenner-everybody-wants-some/82425704/\" data-type\u003d\"link\" data-id\u003d\"https://www.usatoday.com/story/life/movies/2016/03/31/richard-linklater-blake-jenner-everybody-wants-some/82425704/\" target\u003d\"_blank\" rel\u003d\"noreferrer noopener\"\u003e\u0026#34;Everybody Wants Some!!\u0026#34;\u003c/a\u003e). Powell, 35, told USA TODAY that he dove into the script in order to grab a role he didn\u0026#39;t think he\u0026#39;d otherwise get. \u003c/p\u003e"
    }, {
      "type": "text",
      "value": "\u003cp\u003e\u003cspan class\u003d\"exclude-from-newsgate\"\u003e\u003cstrong style\u003d\"margin-right:3px;\"\u003eJoin our Watch Party!  \u003c/strong\u003e\u003ca href\u003d\"https://profile.usatoday.com/newsletters/watch-party/\" target\u003d\"_blank\"\u003eSign up to receive USA TODAY\u0026#39;s movie and TV recommendations right in your inbox\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/span\u003e\u003c/p\u003e"
    }, {
      "type": "asset",
      "value": "74040193007"
    }, {
      "type": "text",
      "value": "\u003cp\u003e\u0026#34;The one thing that you realize about Hollywood is no one\u0026#39;s ever going to give you the role that you\u0026#39;ve dreamed of,\u0026#34; he said. \u0026#34;You have to bake it up yourself.\u0026#34;  \u003c/p\u003e"
    }, {
      "type": "text",
      "value": "\u003cp\u003eLinklater says he was drawn to the project as a way explore the often cliché notion of a killer for hire.\u003c/p\u003e"
    }, {
      "type": "text",
      "value": "\u003cp\u003e\u0026#34;This is the weirdest job ever, being a fake hit man for people who want someone dead,\u0026#34; \u003ca href\u003d\"https://www.statesman.com/story/entertainment/movies/2024/06/06/richard-linklater-hit-man-glen-powell-indie-films-austin-film-society-failure-of-hollywood/73975095007/\" data-type\u003d\"link\" data-id\u003d\"https://www.statesman.com/story/entertainment/movies/2024/06/06/richard-linklater-hit-man-glen-powell-indie-films-austin-film-society-failure-of-hollywood/73975095007/\" target\u003d\"_blank\" rel\u003d\"noreferrer noopener\"\u003eLinklater told the Austin American-Statesman\u003c/a\u003e, part of the USA TODAY Network. \u0026#34;It was more about the myth of the hit man. And these things don’t exist; hit men are created by movies. It’s a pop culture delusion. So I’m kind of interested in that too, about people’s beliefs. I kind of enjoy deconstructing and debunking.\u0026#34;\u003c/p\u003e"
    }, {
      "type": "text",
      "value": "\u003cp\u003eHow much of \u0026#34;Hit Man\u0026#34; is bunk, and how much is based on the real Johnson\u0026#39;s exploits? We explore:\u003c/p\u003e"
    }, {
      "type": "asset",
      "value": "73427001007"
    }, {
      "type": "text",
      "value": "\u003ch2 class\u003d\"presto-h2 wp-block-heading\"\u003eIs Gary Johnson, the hero of Netflix\u0026#39;s \u0026#39;Hit Man,\u0026#39; a real person?\u003c/h2\u003e"
    }, {
      "type": "text",
      "value": "\u003cp\u003eAs depicted in the movie, Johnson was a mild-mannered professor who taught classes in human sexuality and general psychology at a community college. While the film is set in New Orleans, Johnson lived in Houston and worked undercover alongside local law enforcement. Wearing a wire, he would meet with people soliciting hit men and coax out confessions.\u003c/p\u003e"
    }, {
      "type": "text",
      "value": "\u003cp\u003eAccording to Texas Monthly, Johnson was considered \u0026#34;the Laurence Olivier of the field\u0026#34; by his peers. He assisted in the arrests of more than 70 people seeking contract killers: Using aliases such as Mike Caine and Jody Eagle, he posed as everything from country-club sophisticates to rough-and-tumble bikers.\u003c/p\u003e"
    }, {
      "type": "asset",
      "value": "73984623007"
    }, {
      "type": "text",
      "value": "\u003cp\u003e“He’s the perfect chameleon,” Houston lawyer Michael Hinton told Hollandsworth. “He never gets flustered, and he never says the wrong thing. ... He fools them every time.”\u003c/p\u003e"
    }, {
      "type": "text",
      "value": "\u003ch2 class\u003d\"presto-h2 wp-block-heading\"\u003eDid Gary Johnson really fall in love with a woman who wanted him to kill her husband?\u003c/h2\u003e"
    }, {
      "type": "text",
      "value": "\u003cp\u003eEarly in the film, Johnson is contacted by a young woman named Madison Masters (Adria Arjona), or Maddy, who asks him to kill her abusive husband, Ray (Evan Holtzman). Instead, Johnson tells her to keep her money, leave her spouse and start a new life. The two fall into bed together and start a relationship, which quickly turns dangerous when Ray shows up seeking vengeance.\u003c/p\u003e"
    }, {
      "type": "text",
      "value": "\u003cp\u003eAccording to Hollandsworth\u0026#39;s article, there is no evidence that Johnson dated one of his potential \u0026#34;clients.\u0026#34; He does detail instances in which women found Johnson seductive and tried to have sex with him.\u003c/p\u003e"
    }, {
      "type": "text",
      "value": "\u003cp\u003eIn the movie, Johnson is divorced; in reality, Johnson was divorced three times and, according to Texas Monthly, was mostly a soft-spoken loner who enjoyed his cats and reading books by famous philosophers.\u003c/p\u003e"
    }, {
      "type": "text",
      "value": "\u003ch2 class\u003d\"presto-h2 wp-block-heading\"\u003eDid Gary Johnson really talk a client out of ordering a hit?\u003c/h2\u003e"
    }, {
      "type": "text",
      "value": "\u003cp\u003eWhile defense attorneys would argue that Johnson steered his \u0026#34;clients\u0026#34; toward hiring him to commit murder, Johnson maintained that he was trying to help these individuals understand their true desires more clearly. \u003c/p\u003e"
    }, {
      "type": "asset",
      "value": "73185236007"
    }, {
      "type": "text",
      "value": "\u003cp\u003eNever was that more true when he met a young woman at a Starbucks who wanted her boyfriend dead. The “Hit Man” movie uses the unnamed woman\u0026#39;s story as a jumping-off point for the second half of the movie, but the romance was purely fictional.\u003c/p\u003e"
    }, {
      "type": "text",
      "value": "\u003cp\u003eJohnson \u0026#34;learned that she really was the victim of abuse, regularly battered by her boyfriend, too terrified to leave him because of her fear of what he might do if he found her,” Hollandsworth writes. “Instead of setting up a sting to catch the woman and send her off to jail, he decided to help her. He referred her to social service agencies and a therapist to make sure she got proper help so she could leave her boyfriend and get into a women’s shelter.”\u003c/p\u003e"
    }, {
      "type": "text",
      "value": "\u003cp\u003eBut in most cases, even when Johnson would repeatedly ask clients to confirm they wanted to hire a killer, most ultimately said yes, the article says.\u003c/p\u003e"
    }, {
      "type": "text",
      "value": "\u003ch2 class\u003d\"presto-h2 wp-block-heading\"\u003eDid the murders in the Netflix movie \u0026#39;Hit Man\u0026#39; really happen?\u003c/h2\u003e"
    }, {
      "type": "asset",
      "value": "73678332007"
    }, {
      "type": "text",
      "value": "\u003cp\u003eIn \u0026#34;Hit Man,\u0026#34; Powell\u0026#39;s Johnson has fallen in love with Maddy (Arjona) and is determined to ensure that the two have a life together, despite the duo being in serious legal jeopardy. In the movie, Madison has killed her abusive ex-husband, Ray (Holtzman), who tried to take out a hit on her. A police colleague of Johnson\u0026#39;s, Jasper (Austin Amelio), finds out and threatens to turn the couple in unless he is given the million-dollar payment from Ray\u0026#39;s insurance policy.\u003c/p\u003e"
    }, {
      "type": "text",
      "value": "\u003cp\u003eAs Jasper lays out his demands to Gary and Maddy, he suddenly slumps over, as the drugs Maddy has slipped into his drink take effect. While he isn\u0026#39;t quite dead, Gary makes the decision to put a plastic bag over Jasper\u0026#39;s head, killing him. The movie then cuts to a few years down the road, in which, murders be damned, Gary and Maddy have two children and appear to be living happily ever after.\u003c/p\u003e"
    }, {
      "type": "text",
      "value": "\u003cp\u003eThe real Gary Johnson never killed anyone. A title card at the end of movie says Johnson was an animal-loving Buddhist, a Vietnam War veteran and the “chilliest dude imaginable.” He was born in 1947 and died in 2022 before “Hit Man” started shooting, although his cause of death is unknown.  \u003c/p\u003e"
    }, {
      "type": "text",
      "value": "\u003cp\u003e\u003cem\u003eContributing: Brian Truitt\u003c/em\u003e\u003c/p\u003e"
    }]
  }
}

