body:
{
  "body": {
    "desktop": [{
      "type": "asset",
      "value": "12239180002"
    }, {
      "type": "text",
      "value": "\u003cp\u003eI woke up last Sunday to an ominous message from one of my Facebook followers: “Check it out. The fireflies are a month early. An omen?”\u003c/p\u003e"
    }, {
      "type": "text",
      "value": "\u003cp\u003eAfter Googling “fireflies” and coming up empty, I sent her back a message: “What are you talking about?”\u003c/p\u003e"
    }, {
      "type": "text",
      "value": "\u003cp\u003eShe still hasn’t answered. I was intrigued, though. \u003c/p\u003e"
    }, {
      "type": "text",
      "value": "\u003cp\u003eFireflies, incidentally, are actually flying, soft-bodied beetles that light up at night to attract mates. \u003c/p\u003e"
    }, {
      "type": "text",
      "value": "\u003cp\u003e(Oh, the things we do for love.)\u003c/p\u003e"
    }, {
      "type": "text",
      "value": "\u003cp\u003eAn hour after getting her mysterious message, I turned on the TV, scrolled through my guide and noticed that the QVC shopping channel was hosting a viewer favorite  — its annual “Christmas in July” sale!\u003c/p\u003e"
    }, {
      "type": "text",
      "value": "\u003cp\u003eMy first reaction was, “I can’t believe it’s July, already.”\u003c/p\u003e"
    }, {
      "type": "text",
      "value": "\u003cp\u003eMy second reaction was, “Wait a second. It’s still June.”\u003c/p\u003e"
    }, {
      "type": "text",
      "value": "\u003cp\u003eSo…what’s going on?\u003c/p\u003e"
    }, {
      "type": "text",
      "value": "\u003cp\u003eDid QVC move “Christmas in July” to June so it could celebrate Black Friday on the Tuesday after Independence Day?\u003c/p\u003e"
    }, {
      "type": "asset",
      "value": "105445506"
    }, {
      "type": "text",
      "value": "\u003cp\u003eOn an ordinary Sunday at this time of year, I would have said, “Who cares?” and gone out to my patio with a good book and a gallon of sangria.\u003c/p\u003e"
    }, {
      "type": "text",
      "value": "\u003cp\u003eBut, the temperature outside was in the 90s and the humidity was in the 80s and I was clearly not in the mood to leave the cool embrace of my air-conditioner. \u003c/p\u003e"
    }, {
      "type": "text",
      "value": "\u003cp\u003eSo, on a whim, I returned to Google and a did a little search, using the phrase “earlier than usual.”\u003c/p\u003e"
    }, {
      "type": "asset",
      "value": "28682245"
    }, {
      "type": "text",
      "value": "\u003cp\u003eDid you know that, in May, honeybee swarms returned to Colorado a month earlier than usual?\u003c/p\u003e"
    }, {
      "type": "text",
      "value": "\u003cp\u003eNo, I didn’t think you did.\u003c/p\u003e"
    }, {
      "type": "text",
      "value": "\u003cp\u003eAnd did you know that pothole season arrived more than a month earlier than usual in Arkansas, Iowa and Virginia?\u003c/p\u003e"
    }, {
      "type": "text",
      "value": "\u003cp\u003eI’m fairly certain you didn’t. I didn’t know it, either. In fact, I didn’t even know potholes had a season. (It seems to follow periods of freezing and thawing and overlaps with pot roast season.)\u003c/p\u003e"
    }, {
      "type": "text",
      "value": "\u003cp\u003eThe annual Oscars broadcast began one hour earlier than usual this year. (At 7 p.m. instead of 8 p.m.) And the first presidential debate was scheduled for the last Thursday in June, about three months earlier than usual.\u003c/p\u003e"
    }, {
      "type": "text",
      "value": "\u003cp\u003eEven the astronomical summer arrived earlier than usual, beginning last week on June 20, instead of June 21. \u003c/p\u003e"
    }, {
      "type": "text",
      "value": "\u003cp\u003eI have been told that this is the earliest summer solstice since George was in the White House.\u003c/p\u003e"
    }, {
      "type": "text",
      "value": "\u003cp\u003eWashington, not Bush.\u003c/p\u003e"
    }, {
      "type": "text",
      "value": "\u003cp\u003eAnd that was just the tip of the iceberg.\u003c/p\u003e"
    }, {
      "type": "text",
      "value": "\u003cp\u003e\u003cspan class\u003d\"exclude-from-newsgate\"\u003e\u003cstrong style\u003d\"margin-right:3px;\"\u003eErvolino:  \u003c/strong\u003e\u003ca href\u003d\"https://www.northjersey.com/story/news/columnists/2024/06/20/how-to-find-a-new-housekeeper/74127825007/\" target\u003d\"_blank\"\u003eLosing a housekeeper can leave a big, dusty hole in your life\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/span\u003e\u003c/p\u003e"
    }, {
      "type": "text",
      "value": "\u003cp\u003e(For the sake of comparison, I also Googled things that arrive “later than usual” and wound up being redirected to a page on menopause symptoms.)\u003c/p\u003e"
    }, {
      "type": "text",
      "value": "\u003cp\u003eIn Ukraine, wheat and barley crops are being harvested 2-3 weeks earlier than usual. Ditto for Canada’s strawberries, New Zealand’s apples and Iowa’s corn.\u003c/p\u003e"
    }, {
      "type": "text",
      "value": "\u003cp\u003eCertainly, some of these things have been happening because of our mid-June heat wave, but all sorts of strange things have been going on since the first of the year.\u003c/p\u003e"
    }, {
      "type": "text",
      "value": "\u003cp\u003eIn Wisconsin, farmers who usually tap their maple trees in mid-April, started at the end of January.\u003c/p\u003e"
    }, {
      "type": "text",
      "value": "\u003cp\u003eI love real maple syrup, even though it’s a lot more expensive than the corn syrup sold by Log Cabin and whatever name Aunt Jemima is going by these days.\u003c/p\u003e"
    }, {
      "type": "text",
      "value": "\u003cp\u003eBut, since less than half of Iowa’s early corn crop was rated good-to-excellent because of nasty weather, it’s a good bet that the price of corn syrup will be going up, anyway.\u003c/p\u003e"
    }, {
      "type": "text",
      "value": "\u003cp\u003eIn February, a rather famous plant bloomed in California a couple of weeks early. Her name is Mirage. (You name your plants, don’t you?) And she resides in the Osher Rainforest at the California Academy of Sciences.\u003c/p\u003e"
    }, {
      "type": "text",
      "value": "\u003cp\u003eSo, why was she newsworthy?\u003c/p\u003e"
    }, {
      "type": "text",
      "value": "\u003cp\u003eWell, it seems that Mirage is an Amorphophallus titanum, better known as a corpse flower, and her bloom, which usually lasts 3-4 days, is a huge attraction for her fans because, yes, it stinks.\u003c/p\u003e"
    }, {
      "type": "text",
      "value": "\u003cp\u003eTo high heaven.\u003c/p\u003e"
    }, {
      "type": "text",
      "value": "\u003cp\u003eThe plant’s distinctive odor has been described as a combination of rotting flesh, stale onions, week-old fish, garlic and sweaty socks — which is pretty much what my grandmother’s kitchen smelled like.\u003c/p\u003e"
    }, {
      "type": "text",
      "value": "\u003cp\u003eThe aroma, though repellant to most humans, is apparently “irresistible” to beetles and flies, which the corpse plant relies on for pollination. As to why it opened earlier than usual this year, no one seems to know.\u003c/p\u003e"
    }, {
      "type": "text",
      "value": "\u003cp\u003eCertainly, many plants have bloomed earlier than expected because of the unseasonably warm weather we’ve been having. But, the Osher Rainforest is indoors, contained within a dome constructed inside the Academy.\u003c/p\u003e"
    }, {
      "type": "text",
      "value": "\u003cp\u003eSome folks believe that all of these things coming earlier rather than later, have something to do with La Nina, El Nino, climate change, the Wicked Witch of the West, changes in the earth’s orbit and, my personal favorite, God looking at us on his big screen TV and saying, “Let’s get this over with.”\u003c/p\u003e"
    }, {
      "type": "text",
      "value": "\u003cp\u003eI honestly don’t know.\u003c/p\u003e"
    }, {
      "type": "text",
      "value": "\u003cp\u003e(A closing note: I had planned to do a column on all this for Labor Day weekend. But, for some reason, I did it earlier.)\u003c/p\u003e"
    }],
