body:
{
  "body": {
    "desktop": [{
      "type": "asset",
      "value": "1297748002"
    }, {
      "type": "text",
      "value": "\u003cp\u003eThere\u0026#39;s a moment of truth, for every young musician.\u003c/p\u003e"
    }, {
      "type": "text",
      "value": "\u003cp\u003eBetween the time they first touch a violin, at perhaps age 5, and the time they make their debut at Carnegie Hall, there\u0026#39;s a moment when they\u0026#39;re tempted to quit. \u003c/p\u003e"
    }, {
      "type": "text",
      "value": "\u003cp\u003eTrue, for every kid. And truer still for young musicians of color — who often get small encouragement from a culture that, historically, doesn\u0026#39;t see the New York Philharmonic as a career goal for them. By the time they reach high school, many are ready to throw in the towel.\u003c/p\u003e"
    }, {
      "type": "text",
      "value": "\u003cp\u003eMarie Dillard, the 18-year-old creator of \u003ca href\u003d\"https://www.mariesdillard.com/the-crescendo-lab/\" data-type\u003d\"link\" data-id\u003d\"https://www.mariesdillard.com/the-crescendo-lab/\"\u003eThe Crescendo Lab\u003c/a\u003e, knows. She might have been one of them.\u003c/p\u003e"
    }, {
      "type": "asset",
      "value": "74064789007"
    }, {
      "type": "text",
      "value": "\u003cp\u003e\u0026#34;I just felt left out,\u0026#34; she said. \u0026#34;There was no reason to see myself in classical music.\u0026#34;\u003c/p\u003e"
    }, {
      "type": "text",
      "value": "\u003cp\u003eThat\u0026#39;s the reason Dillard, a lifelong Englewood resident, created a program to encourage those kids to stay on track. She just received $5,000 from the \u003ca href\u003d\"https://www.prudential.com/links/about/emergingvisionaries\" data-type\u003d\"link\" data-id\u003d\"https://www.prudential.com/links/about/emergingvisionaries\"\u003ePrudential Emerging Visionaries program\u003c/a\u003e to take her Crescendo Lab to the next level.\u003c/p\u003e"
    }, {
      "type": "text",
      "value": "\u003cp\u003e\u0026#34;It\u0026#39;s about empowering them and inspiring them to continue to play classical music, and just stick with it,\u0026#34; said Dillard, herself a violist. \u003c/p\u003e"
    }, {
      "type": "text",
      "value": "\u003cp\u003e\u0026#34;When I got to high school I saw so many kids, especially Black and brown kids, who played classical music in middle school, and quit before high school,\u0026#34; she said. \u003c/p\u003e"
    }, {
      "type": "text",
      "value": "\u003ch2 class\u003d\"presto-h2 exclude-from-newsgate wp-block-heading\"\u003eGrown from seed\u003c/h2\u003e"
    }, {
      "type": "text",
      "value": "\u003cp\u003eLast summer, using $750 in seed money she got from the \u003ca href\u003d\"https://www.beallyou.org/\" data-type\u003d\"link\" data-id\u003d\"https://www.beallyou.org/\"\u003eBe All You\u003c/a\u003e entrepreneurship program, she began The Crescendo Lab with five students, ages 5 to 12, whom she mentored out of an office in Englewood. It\u0026#39;s an offshoot of her own nonprofit Persistent Endeavors Foundation.\u003c/p\u003e"
    }, {
      "type": "asset",
      "value": "74064830007"
    }, {
      "type": "text",
      "value": "\u003cp\u003e\u0026#34;It\u0026#39;s a classical music mentoring program that focuses on Black and brown students,\u0026#34; she said. \u0026#34;With a sort of historic lens. I will mentor them, and show them people who look like them. When I started playing classical music, I didn\u0026#39;t see a lot of people who looked like me.\u0026#34;\u003c/p\u003e"
    }, {
      "type": "text",
      "value": "\u003cp\u003eThe five-week, once-a-week course was not just about teaching these kids to be better musicians. It\u0026#39;s also — and crucially — about teaching them that people of color have a place in classical music. \u003c/p\u003e"
    }, {
      "type": "asset",
      "value": "74059162007"
    }, {
      "type": "text",
      "value": "\u003cp\u003eHow, after all, can you imagine doing something that you\u0026#39;ve never seen anyone else like you do?\u003c/p\u003e"
    }, {
      "type": "text",
      "value": "\u003cp\u003e\u0026#34;I\u0026#39;d never heard of Black people composing classical music,\u0026#34; she said. \u0026#34;That was something I never really thought of. If you think of a Black composer, you think of jazz. Black people have sort of been put into this category. \u0026#39;This is your music.\u0026#39; \u0026#34;\u003c/p\u003e"
    }, {
      "type": "text",
      "value": "\u003cp\u003eThere are, of course, plenty of Black classical composers — though they tend to be overlooked in standard survey courses. Joseph Bologne, the 18th century French violinist and composer, subject of the 2022 biopic \u0026#34;Chevalier,\u0026#34; is one. Dillard teaches her students about him. Florence Price, who composed over 300 works including four symphonies, is another. Then there\u0026#39;s Scott Joplin, whose \u0026#34;ragtime\u0026#34; pieces were actually elegant concert works (he also wrote two operas). \u003c/p\u003e"
    }, {
      "type": "text",
      "value": "\u003cp\u003eThere are plenty of others: William Grant Still, Ulysses Kay, Louis Moreau Gottschalk, James P. Johnson (he did concert works like \u0026#34;Yamacraw,\u0026#34; in addition to composing jazz), Terence Blanchard, and more.\u003c/p\u003e"
    }, {
      "type": "text",
      "value": "\u003ch2 class\u003d\"presto-h2 exclude-from-newsgate wp-block-heading\"\u003eMusic appreciation\u003c/h2\u003e"
    }, {
      "type": "text",
      "value": "\u003cp\u003eSuch names tend not to be so well known, by white \u003cem\u003eor\u003c/em\u003e Black kids. \u0026#34;Anytime I hear of any programs that had to do with Black kids and music, it only had to do with hop-hop, something like that,\u0026#34; she said. \u0026#34;That makes sense. I don\u0026#39;t blame them for that. But at the same time, I never had anyone saying, \u0026#39;Hey, I\u0026#39;m a Black person, I play classical music.\u0026#39; \u0026#34;\u003c/p\u003e"
    }, {
      "type": "text",
      "value": "\u003cp\u003eShe herself was lucky: she grew up in a house where both parents had been musicians. Mom played violin. Dad played sax and piano.\u003c/p\u003e"
    }, {
      "type": "text",
      "value": "\u003cp\u003e\u0026#34;Growing up, I was exposed to all kinds of music,\u0026#34; she said. \u0026#34;I started at a very young age. It wasn\u0026#39;t until 4 that I picked up an instrument.\u0026#34;\u003c/p\u003e"
    }, {
      "type": "text",
      "value": "\u003cp\u003eIt was the violin: Her mother had a theory about it.  Rubbing the bow on the strings with one hand, while fingering with the other, brought both sides of the brain into play. \u0026#34;Your brain is crossing hemispheres,\u0026#34; Dillard said. \u0026#34;There\u0026#39;s something fundamental to learning that happens to your brain.\u0026#34;\u003c/p\u003e"
    }, {
      "type": "text",
      "value": "\u003cp\u003eIn middle school (Elisabeth Morrow) she switched to viola, which has been her go-to instrument ever since. She recently graduated from St. Andrews, a private school in Delaware. She\u0026#39;ll be entering the University of Pennsylvania in the fall. But wherever she goes, she\u0026#39;ll take The Crescendo Lab with her.\u003c/p\u003e"
    }, {
      "type": "asset",
      "value": "74064793007"
    }, {
      "type": "text",
      "value": "\u003cp\u003e\u0026#34;I\u0026#39;m thinking of a recorded series of short-form videos, where I talk about the lives of Black and brown composers,\u0026#34; she said. \u0026#34;I want to grow an online curriculum. And I want to interview Black and brown musicians today, who are in this area. There\u0026#39;s so much more to do.\u0026#34;\u003c/p\u003e"
    }, {
      "type": "text",
      "value": "\u003cp\u003eVisit: mariesdillard.com/the-crescendo-lab\u003c/p\u003e"
    }],
    "mobile": [{
      "type": "asset",
      "value": "1297748002"
    }, {
      "type": "text",
      "value": "\u003cp\u003eThere\u0026#39;s a moment of truth, for every young musician.\u003c/p\u003e"
    }, {
      "type": "text",
      "value": "\u003cp\u003eBetween the time they first touch a violin, at perhaps age 5, and the time they make their debut at Carnegie Hall, there\u0026#39;s a moment when they\u0026#39;re tempted to quit. \u003c/p\u003e"
    }, {
      "type": "text",
      "value": "\u003cp\u003eTrue, for every kid. And truer still for young musicians of color — who often get small encouragement from a culture that, historically, doesn\u0026#39;t see the New York Philharmonic as a career goal for them. By the time they reach high school, many are ready to throw in the towel.\u003c/p\u003e"
    }, {
      "type": "text",
      "value": "\u003cp\u003eMarie Dillard, the 18-year-old creator of \u003ca href\u003d\"https://www.mariesdillard.com/the-crescendo-lab/\" data-type\u003d\"link\" data-id\u003d\"https://www.mariesdillard.com/the-crescendo-lab/\"\u003eThe Crescendo Lab\u003c/a\u003e, knows. She might have been one of them.\u003c/p\u003e"
    }, {
      "type": "asset",
      "value": "74064789007"
    }, {
      "type": "text",
      "value": "\u003cp\u003e\u0026#34;I just felt left out,\u0026#34; she said. \u0026#34;There was no reason to see myself in classical music.\u0026#34;\u003c/p\u003e"
    }, {
      "type": "text",
      "value": "\u003cp\u003eThat\u0026#39;s the reason Dillard, a lifelong Englewood resident, created a program to encourage those kids to stay on track. She just received $5,000 from the \u003ca href\u003d\"https://www.prudential.com/links/about/emergingvisionaries\" data-type\u003d\"link\" data-id\u003d\"https://www.prudential.com/links/about/emergingvisionaries\"\u003ePrudential Emerging Visionaries program\u003c/a\u003e to take her Crescendo Lab to the next level.\u003c/p\u003e"
    }, {
      "type": "text",
      "value": "\u003cp\u003e\u0026#34;It\u0026#39;s about empowering them and inspiring them to continue to play classical music, and just stick with it,\u0026#34; said Dillard, herself a violist. \u003c/p\u003e"
    }, {
      "type": "text",
      "value": "\u003cp\u003e\u0026#34;When I got to high school I saw so many kids, especially Black and brown kids, who played classical music in middle school, and quit before high school,\u0026#34; she said. \u003c/p\u003e"
    }, {
      "type": "text",
      "value": "\u003ch2 class\u003d\"presto-h2 exclude-from-newsgate wp-block-heading\"\u003eGrown from seed\u003c/h2\u003e"
    }, {
      "type": "text",
      "value": "\u003cp\u003eLast summer, using $750 in seed money she got from the \u003ca href\u003d\"https://www.beallyou.org/\" data-type\u003d\"link\" data-id\u003d\"https://www.beallyou.org/\"\u003eBe All You\u003c/a\u003e entrepreneurship program, she began The Crescendo Lab with five students, ages 5 to 12, whom she mentored out of an office in Englewood. It\u0026#39;s an offshoot of her own nonprofit Persistent Endeavors Foundation.\u003c/p\u003e"
    }, {
      "type": "asset",
      "value": "74064830007"
    }, {
      "type": "text",
      "value": "\u003cp\u003e\u0026#34;It\u0026#39;s a classical music mentoring program that focuses on Black and brown students,\u0026#34; she said. \u0026#34;With a sort of historic lens. I will mentor them, and show them people who look like them. When I started playing classical music, I didn\u0026#39;t see a lot of people who looked like me.\u0026#34;\u003c/p\u003e"
    }, {
      "type": "text",
      "value": "\u003cp\u003eThe five-week, once-a-week course was not just about teaching these kids to be better musicians. It\u0026#39;s also — and crucially — about teaching them that people of color have a place in classical music. \u003c/p\u003e"
    }, {
      "type": "asset",
      "value": "74059162007"
    }, {
      "type": "text",
      "value": "\u003cp\u003eHow, after all, can you imagine doing something that you\u0026#39;ve never seen anyone else like you do?\u003c/p\u003e"
    }, {
      "type": "text",
      "value": "\u003cp\u003e\u0026#34;I\u0026#39;d never heard of Black people composing classical music,\u0026#34; she said. \u0026#34;That was something I never really thought of. If you think of a Black composer, you think of jazz. Black people have sort of been put into this category. \u0026#39;This is your music.\u0026#39; \u0026#34;\u003c/p\u003e"
    }, {
      "type": "text",
      "value": "\u003cp\u003eThere are, of course, plenty of Black classical composers — though they tend to be overlooked in standard survey courses. Joseph Bologne, the 18th century French violinist and composer, subject of the 2022 biopic \u0026#34;Chevalier,\u0026#34; is one. Dillard teaches her students about him. Florence Price, who composed over 300 works including four symphonies, is another. Then there\u0026#39;s Scott Joplin, whose \u0026#34;ragtime\u0026#34; pieces were actually elegant concert works (he also wrote two operas). \u003c/p\u003e"
    }, {
      "type": "text",
      "value": "\u003cp\u003eThere are plenty of others: William Grant Still, Ulysses Kay, Louis Moreau Gottschalk, James P. Johnson (he did concert works like \u0026#34;Yamacraw,\u0026#34; in addition to composing jazz), Terence Blanchard, and more.\u003c/p\u003e"
    }, {
      "type": "text",
      "value": "\u003ch2 class\u003d\"presto-h2 exclude-from-newsgate wp-block-heading\"\u003eMusic appreciation\u003c/h2\u003e"
    }, {
      "type": "text",
      "value": "\u003cp\u003eSuch names tend not to be so well known, by white \u003cem\u003eor\u003c/em\u003e Black kids. \u0026#34;Anytime I hear of any programs that had to do with Black kids and music, it only had to do with hop-hop, something like that,\u0026#34; she said. \u0026#34;That makes sense. I don\u0026#39;t blame them for that. But at the same time, I never had anyone saying, \u0026#39;Hey, I\u0026#39;m a Black person, I play classical music.\u0026#39; \u0026#34;\u003c/p\u003e"
    }, {
      "type": "text",
      "value": "\u003cp\u003eShe herself was lucky: she grew up in a house where both parents had been musicians. Mom played violin. Dad played sax and piano.\u003c/p\u003e"
    }, {
      "type": "text",
      "value": "\u003cp\u003e\u0026#34;Growing up, I was exposed to all kinds of music,\u0026#34; she said. \u0026#34;I started at a very young age. It wasn\u0026#39;t until 4 that I picked up an instrument.\u0026#34;\u003c/p\u003e"
    }, {
      "type": "text",
      "value": "\u003cp\u003eIt was the violin: Her mother had a theory about it.  Rubbing the bow on the strings with one hand, while fingering with the other, brought both sides of the brain into play. \u0026#34;Your brain is crossing hemispheres,\u0026#34; Dillard said. \u0026#34;There\u0026#39;s something fundamental to learning that happens to your brain.\u0026#34;\u003c/p\u003e"
    }, {
      "type": "text",
      "value": "\u003cp\u003eIn middle school (Elisabeth Morrow) she switched to viola, which has been her go-to instrument ever since. She recently graduated from St. Andrews, a private school in Delaware. She\u0026#39;ll be entering the University of Pennsylvania in the fall. But wherever she goes, she\u0026#39;ll take The Crescendo Lab with her.\u003c/p\u003e"
    }, {
      "type": "asset",
      "value": "74064793007"
    }, {
      "type": "text",
      "value": "\u003cp\u003e\u0026#34;I\u0026#39;m thinking of a recorded series of short-form videos, where I talk about the lives of Black and brown composers,\u0026#34; she said. \u0026#34;I want to grow an online curriculum. And I want to interview Black and brown musicians today, who are in this area. There\u0026#39;s so much more to do.\u0026#34;\u003c/p\u003e"
    }, {
      "type": "text",
      "value": "\u003cp\u003eVisit: mariesdillard.com/the-crescendo-lab\u003c/p\u003e"
    }]
  }
}

