body:
{
  "body": {
    "desktop": [{
      "type": "asset",
      "value": "11382492002"
    }, {
      "type": "text",
      "value": "\u003cp\u003eA Bergenfield school district employee said she is owed back wages and was unfairly retaliated against after she filed gender discrimination complaints against other administrators.\u003c/p\u003e"
    }, {
      "type": "text",
      "value": "\u003cp\u003eLigia Alberto said she worked as a school counselor from 2010 to 2015, as the director of testing, teaching and learning from 2015 to 2018 and has been the director of the alternative school, testing and data analysis since 2018.\u003c/p\u003e"
    }, {
      "type": "text",
      "value": "\u003cp\u003eIn a lawsuit filed against the district in Bergen County Superior Court, Alberto said she was subjected to \u0026#34;gender discriminatory comments,\u0026#34; shown less favor than male colleagues and nothing was done by the administration when she brought these issues up.\u003c/p\u003e"
    }, {
      "type": "text",
      "value": "\u003cp\u003eSuperintendent Christopher Tully, Bergenfield High School Principal Jim Fasano and the board of education are all named in the lawsuit in addition to the district. Tully said the district does not comment on personnel and litigation.\u003c/p\u003e"
    }, {
      "type": "text",
      "value": "\u003cp\u003eAccording to the lawsuit, Alberto \u0026#34;performed her duties in an exemplary fashion\u0026#34; and had positive performance reviews which led to her promotion as the director of the district\u0026#39;s alternative school. The suit said the school was \u0026#34;severely mismanaged, disorganized and failing to provide an appropriate education for students\u0026#34; before she took over and revamped it. \u003c/p\u003e"
    }, {
      "type": "text",
      "value": "\u003cp\u003eAlberto said Fasano continually referred to her as \u0026#34;young woman\u0026#34; even though she asked him to refer to her as Dr. Alberto. She said she complained to Darlene Markman, the assistant superintendent, and Tully about Fasano\u0026#39;s comments and no action was taken. The lawsuit said Fasano would refer to Alberto\u0026#39;s male colleagues by their name and not \u0026#34;young man\u0026#34; and he would show them favoritism.\u003c/p\u003e"
    }, {
      "type": "text",
      "value": "\u003cp\u003e\u003cspan class\u003d\"exclude-from-newsgate\"\u003e\u003cstrong style\u003d\"margin-right:3px;\"\u003eNJ news \u003c/strong\u003e\u003ca href\u003d\"https://www.northjersey.com/story/news/new-jersey/2024/06/17/george-norcross-iii-indicted-nj-racketeering/74126229007/\" target\u003d\"_blank\"\u003eGeorge Norcross III, South Jersey’s Democratic power broker, faces racketeering charges\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/span\u003e\u003c/p\u003e"
    }, {
      "type": "text",
      "value": "\u003cp\u003eOn June 16, 2022, Alberto filed an affirmative action complaint, alleging gender discrimination and she said it resulted in Fasano retaliating against her by ignoring her and \u0026#34;treating her like a pariah.\u0026#34;\u003c/p\u003e"
    }, {
      "type": "text",
      "value": "\u003cp\u003eIn July 2022, Alberto requested to go on a sabbatical leave which was part of the collective bargaining agreement.\u003c/p\u003e"
    }, {
      "type": "text",
      "value": "\u003cp\u003eAlberto said her sabbatical aligned with district goals and told Tully she would be teaching and conducting research at Felician University in Rutherford during her leave. The lawsuit said Alberto agreed to delay her sabbatical until January 2023 to find a replacement for her.\u003c/p\u003e"
    }, {
      "type": "text",
      "value": "\u003cp\u003eAccording to the lawsuit, district policy would pay half of an administrator\u0026#39;s salary for one year and administrators are not required to perform work for the district or school board while on sabbatical.\u003c/p\u003e"
    }, {
      "type": "text",
      "value": "\u003cp\u003eAlberto said in September 2022, Tully called her into his office to tell her the board had approved her sabbatical from January 2023 until January 2024 and gave her a sidebar agreement document that he said had to be signed for her to go on leave. The agreement document had a provision that required Alberto to continue her duties as the district\u0026#39;s testing coordinator and work with the assistant superintendent for curriculum and instruction while on leave.\u003c/p\u003e"
    }, {
      "type": "text",
      "value": "\u003cp\u003eThe suit accuses Tully of \u0026#34;falsely representing\u0026#34; that Alberto had to sign the agreement to go on sabbatical, which she did under \u0026#34;false representations.\u0026#34; She said she signed the agreement believing she would be \u0026#34;fully compensated\u0026#34; for working as the district testing coordinator and for working with the assistant superintendent for curriculum and instruction.\u003c/p\u003e"
    }, {
      "type": "text",
      "value": "\u003cp\u003eAccording to the lawsuit, Alberto was \u0026#34;purposefully singled out\u0026#34; because she was a woman and she filed the discrimination complaint against Fasano and Tully. She said she wasn\u0026#39;t compensated fully for continuing to work despite being on sabbatical and was required to attend the district\u0026#39;s administrative retreat in August 2023 but was not compensated.\u003c/p\u003e"
    }, {
      "type": "text",
      "value": "\u003cp\u003eAlberto said she complained to Tully that she should be compensated for continuing to work with the district while out on sabbatical. The lawsuit said in October 2023, Tully \u0026#34;threatened\u0026#34; Alberto if she didn\u0026#39;t continue to do her job as as the district testing coordinator and the collaborative work while on leave and without additional compensation, she would be fired. According to the lawsuit, the sidebar agreement ended in June 2023.\u003c/p\u003e"
    }, {
      "type": "text",
      "value": "\u003cp\u003eAlberto filed a second discrimination charge in October 2023 which she said resulted in more retaliatory actions including being told by Tully in November 2023 that the district would no longer pay her any money for the remainder of her sabbatical and she would be placed on leave without pay because of the discrimination charge. The lawsuit said Alberto was further threatened by Tully that \u0026#34;her conduct\u0026#34; would be considered as a part of her annual evaluation for 2023-2024.\u003c/p\u003e"
    }, {
      "type": "text",
      "value": "\u003cp\u003eThe suit said no actions were taken and nothing was investigated despite Alberto\u0026#39;s complaints. She said the district continued to single her out, failing to pay her for going to the Middle States Annual Commissioner\u0026#39;s Meeting in Philadephia, leaving her out of the district\u0026#39;s annual Christmas celebration and failing to pay her and credit her vacation days. The lawsuit stated Alberto was owed $100,000 and she was dropped from the pension system for a year, which made her lose benefits.\u003c/p\u003e"
    }, {
      "type": "text",
      "value": "\u003cp\u003eAccording to the lawsuit, Alberto is still entitled to a one-year sabbatical since she worked for 10 months and the district created a hostile work environment and caused her \u0026#34;severe emotional distress.\u0026#34; The suit said the stress from the situation has caused \u0026#34;uncontrollable eye twitching and bouts of severe emotional trauma and anxiety.\u0026#34; She sought a medical leave of absence from Jan. 15 until Feb. 26 because of panic attacks caused by what happened.\u003c/p\u003e"
    }],
