body:
{
  "body": {
    "desktop": [{
      "type": "asset",
      "value": "4273281002"
    }, {
      "type": "text",
      "value": "\u003cp\u003eTeterboro is more than just an airport to the dozens of people who call the tiny Bergen County borough home. \u003c/p\u003e"
    }, {
      "type": "text",
      "value": "\u003cp\u003eTeterboro ranks among the smallest towns in New Jersey. According to Teterboro\u0026#39;s website, it occupies a 1.1 square mile area with only 61 residents (as of the 2020 census). This area not only includes the airport, but also the Bergen County Technical School and Bergen County Animal Shelter, and major businesses like Walmart and Costco.\u003c/p\u003e"
    }, {
      "type": "text",
      "value": "\u003cp\u003eAccording to the Port Authority of New York’s website, “Teterboro is the oldest operating airport in the New York and New Jersey metropolitan area,” with the airport operating since 1919. Teterboro was established in 1917, so the town had only lived without an airport for two years.\u003c/p\u003e"
    }, {
      "type": "text",
      "value": "\u003ch2 class\u003d\"presto-h2 exclude-from-newsgate wp-block-heading\"\u003eLiving in Teterboro\u003c/h2\u003e"
    }, {
      "type": "text",
      "value": "\u003cp\u003eFinding the residential part of Teterboro can be tricky. It may seem like Teterboro but actually be part of neighboring towns like Moonachie, Hasbrouck Heights or South Hackensack. Some of the few Teterboro residents live on Vincent Place and Huyler Street.\u003c/p\u003e"
    }, {
      "type": "text",
      "value": "\u003cp\u003eVincent Place has a complex of townhouses, and of the many doors knocked on at Vincent Place, only two residents agreed to talk about their life in Teterboro.\u003c/p\u003e"
    }, {
      "type": "text",
      "value": "\u003cp\u003eTo some noise is a factor while others found it tranquil. When asked what it\u0026#39;s like living there, Paul Pereira, a resident of four years, voiced some complaints about noise. But it wasn\u0026#39;t about the airport, but a nearby gun range. \u003c/p\u003e"
    }, {
      "type": "asset",
      "value": "1032839002"
    }, {
      "type": "text",
      "value": "\u003cp\u003e“Every Sunday at 11, you can set your watch on it, they’re blowing off bombs over there [at the gun range] and I’m finding bullets in my backyard,” Pereira said. \u003c/p\u003e"
    }, {
      "type": "text",
      "value": "\u003cp\u003ePereira said he has no problem with the airplane noise, but the gun range is another story. On the positive side, Pereira did say the town takes good care of its residents in other ways.\u003c/p\u003e"
    }, {
      "type": "text",
      "value": "\u003cp\u003eOn the other hand, resident Rich Ramessar could not think of one complaint he had about his living situation. Ramessar thinks the low population is simply due to the lack of housing.\u003c/p\u003e"
    }, {
      "type": "text",
      "value": "\u003cp\u003e“[Living in Teterboro has] been great,” Ramessar said. “I’ve been here for six years and it’s been quiet and nice.”\u003c/p\u003e"
    }, {
      "type": "text",
      "value": "\u003cp\u003eRamessar does construction work and believes that the townhouses were built with special soundproofing to help with the noise from the airport.\u003c/p\u003e"
    }, {
      "type": "text",
      "value": "\u003cp\u003eOn Huyler Street lives Rona Al shaikh Mahmoud and Husan Rajeb, mother and son.\u003c/p\u003e"
    }, {
      "type": "text",
      "value": "\u003cp\u003eThe pair have been living in the house for 13 years, and moved in after a suggestion from Al shaikh Mahmoud’s brother-in-law and now neighbor. They also live down the block from their cousins.\u003c/p\u003e"
    }, {
      "type": "text",
      "value": "\u003cp\u003eRajeb, a sophomore at Don Bosco High School, reminisced about the years prior.\u003c/p\u003e"
    }, {
      "type": "text",
      "value": "\u003cp\u003e“A few years ago, before Walmart and Costco and all those other stores opened up, these streets were pretty quiet,” Rejeb said. “Now, they’re pretty chaotic.”\u003c/p\u003e"
    }, {
      "type": "text",
      "value": "\u003cp\u003eRajeb does not feel that the airplanes are annoying, and feels used to the noise. Rajeb loves being close to his favorite restaurants, especially Chipotle and Chick-fil-A.\u003c/p\u003e"
    }, {
      "type": "text",
      "value": "\u003cp\u003eAl shaikh Mahmoud said Teterboro is a good location, especially with highways all over and easy access. Al shaikh Mahmoud is also fond of the town and people surrounding her.\u003c/p\u003e"
    }, {
      "type": "text",
      "value": "\u003cp\u003e“We all are friends here,” she said. “I like my neighborhood, and they [neighbors] are all very nice people.”\u003c/p\u003e"
    }],
    "mobile": [{
      "type": "asset",
      "value": "4273281002"
    }, {
      "type": "text",
      "value": "\u003cp\u003eTeterboro is more than just an airport to the dozens of people who call the tiny Bergen County borough home. \u003c/p\u003e"
    }, {
      "type": "text",
      "value": "\u003cp\u003eTeterboro ranks among the smallest towns in New Jersey. According to Teterboro\u0026#39;s website, it occupies a 1.1 square mile area with only 61 residents (as of the 2020 census). This area not only includes the airport, but also the Bergen County Technical School and Bergen County Animal Shelter, and major businesses like Walmart and Costco.\u003c/p\u003e"
    }, {
      "type": "text",
      "value": "\u003cp\u003eAccording to the Port Authority of New York’s website, “Teterboro is the oldest operating airport in the New York and New Jersey metropolitan area,” with the airport operating since 1919. Teterboro was established in 1917, so the town had only lived without an airport for two years.\u003c/p\u003e"
    }, {
      "type": "text",
      "value": "\u003ch2 class\u003d\"presto-h2 exclude-from-newsgate wp-block-heading\"\u003eLiving in Teterboro\u003c/h2\u003e"
    }, {
      "type": "text",
      "value": "\u003cp\u003eFinding the residential part of Teterboro can be tricky. It may seem like Teterboro but actually be part of neighboring towns like Moonachie, Hasbrouck Heights or South Hackensack. Some of the few Teterboro residents live on Vincent Place and Huyler Street.\u003c/p\u003e"
    }, {
      "type": "text",
      "value": "\u003cp\u003eVincent Place has a complex of townhouses, and of the many doors knocked on at Vincent Place, only two residents agreed to talk about their life in Teterboro.\u003c/p\u003e"
    }, {
      "type": "text",
      "value": "\u003cp\u003eTo some noise is a factor while others found it tranquil. When asked what it\u0026#39;s like living there, Paul Pereira, a resident of four years, voiced some complaints about noise. But it wasn\u0026#39;t about the airport, but a nearby gun range. \u003c/p\u003e"
    }, {
      "type": "asset",
      "value": "1032839002"
    }, {
      "type": "text",
      "value": "\u003cp\u003e“Every Sunday at 11, you can set your watch on it, they’re blowing off bombs over there [at the gun range] and I’m finding bullets in my backyard,” Pereira said. \u003c/p\u003e"
    }, {
      "type": "text",
      "value": "\u003cp\u003ePereira said he has no problem with the airplane noise, but the gun range is another story. On the positive side, Pereira did say the town takes good care of its residents in other ways.\u003c/p\u003e"
    }, {
      "type": "text",
      "value": "\u003cp\u003eOn the other hand, resident Rich Ramessar could not think of one complaint he had about his living situation. Ramessar thinks the low population is simply due to the lack of housing.\u003c/p\u003e"
    }, {
      "type": "text",
      "value": "\u003cp\u003e“[Living in Teterboro has] been great,” Ramessar said. “I’ve been here for six years and it’s been quiet and nice.”\u003c/p\u003e"
    }, {
      "type": "text",
      "value": "\u003cp\u003eRamessar does construction work and believes that the townhouses were built with special soundproofing to help with the noise from the airport.\u003c/p\u003e"
    }, {
      "type": "text",
      "value": "\u003cp\u003eOn Huyler Street lives Rona Al shaikh Mahmoud and Husan Rajeb, mother and son.\u003c/p\u003e"
    }, {
      "type": "text",
      "value": "\u003cp\u003eThe pair have been living in the house for 13 years, and moved in after a suggestion from Al shaikh Mahmoud’s brother-in-law and now neighbor. They also live down the block from their cousins.\u003c/p\u003e"
    }, {
      "type": "text",
      "value": "\u003cp\u003eRajeb, a sophomore at Don Bosco High School, reminisced about the years prior.\u003c/p\u003e"
    }, {
      "type": "text",
      "value": "\u003cp\u003e“A few years ago, before Walmart and Costco and all those other stores opened up, these streets were pretty quiet,” Rejeb said. “Now, they’re pretty chaotic.”\u003c/p\u003e"
    }, {
      "type": "text",
      "value": "\u003cp\u003eRajeb does not feel that the airplanes are annoying, and feels used to the noise. Rajeb loves being close to his favorite restaurants, especially Chipotle and Chick-fil-A.\u003c/p\u003e"
    }, {
      "type": "text",
      "value": "\u003cp\u003eAl shaikh Mahmoud said Teterboro is a good location, especially with highways all over and easy access. Al shaikh Mahmoud is also fond of the town and people surrounding her.\u003c/p\u003e"
    }, {
      "type": "text",
      "value": "\u003cp\u003e“We all are friends here,” she said. “I like my neighborhood, and they [neighbors] are all very nice people.”\u003c/p\u003e"
    }]
  }
}

