body:
{
  "body": {
    "desktop": [{
      "type": "asset",
      "value": "73970952007"
    }, {
      "type": "text",
      "value": "\u003cp\u003eWYCKOFF — In a rare departure from party-line politics, voters bypassed the official GOP nominees to elect three alternate \u0026#34;Republicans for Wyckoff\u0026#34; committee candidates in Tuesday\u0026#39;s primary to run for seats on the Township Committee in November.   \u003c/p\u003e"
    }, {
      "type": "text",
      "value": "\u003cp\u003eIncumbent Thomas Madigan won his bid to run for a fourth term with newcomer Mae Bogdansky and Roger Lane, running for the two years remaining in the term of Committeeman Scott Fisher who resigned in December. Lane was appointed to the vacancy in January.\u003c/p\u003e"
    }, {
      "type": "text",
      "value": "\u003cp\u003eNot even the inclusion of Wyckoff County Committee Chair Drita McNamara on the slate with two-term Committeeman Timothy Shanley and newcomer Christopher Joachim was enough to sway voters during a presidential election year when placement in the authorized \u0026#34;Republicans for Responsible Government\u0026#34; GOP column was considered essential to political success.\u003c/p\u003e"
    }, {
      "type": "asset",
      "value": "73415016007"
    }, {
      "type": "text",
      "value": "\u003cp\u003eFor the two three-year terms, the tallies were Madigan with 1,150 votes, Bogdansky with 1,147 votes, Shanley with 928 votes and McNamara with 873 votes. For the two-year unexpired term, Lane had 1,117 votes and Joachim had 914 votes. No Democrats filed to run against them for November\u0026#39;s Election Day. Ironically, Madigan and Shanley ran together in 2018 and 2021.\u003c/p\u003e"
    }, {
      "type": "text",
      "value": "\u003cp\u003e\u0026#34;Thank you Wyckoff neighbors for your support and reaffirmation of our focus on Wyckoff, not the failed county cronies!\u0026#34; Madigan said on Wednesday.\u003c/p\u003e"
    }, {
      "type": "text",
      "value": "\u003cp\u003e\u0026#34;I want to thank the voters of Wyckoff for putting their faith in me. Now it’s time to bring all Wyckoff Republicans together and I pledge to work toward that end,\u0026#34; Lane said on Wednesday.\u003c/p\u003e"
    }, {
      "type": "text",
      "value": "\u003cp\u003eBogdansky, vice president of the Wyckoff Republican Club, said she was \u0026#34;deeply concerned that our Wyckoff Republican Party was tested,\u0026#34; adding \u0026#34;we work together to unite, not only as Republicans, but also as neighbors.\u0026#34;\u003c/p\u003e"
    }, {
      "type": "text",
      "value": "\u003cp\u003e\u003cspan class\u003d\"exclude-from-newsgate\"\u003e\u003cstrong style\u003d\"margin-right:3px;\"\u003eWYCKOFF PRIMARY \u003c/strong\u003e\u003ca href\u003d\"https://www.northjersey.com/story/news/bergen/2022/10/21/politics-top-concern-in-ramapo-indian-hills-school-board-races/69574942007/\" target\u003d\"_blank\"\u003ePolitics are a top concern in Ramapo Indian Hills nonpartisan school board races\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/span\u003e\u003c/p\u003e"
    }, {
      "type": "text",
      "value": "\u003cp\u003e\u0026#34;Thank you to the many friends who pushed for us in these past few months and to the Wyckoff voters for their support and faith in us,\u0026#34; Bogdansky said.\u003c/p\u003e"
    }, {
      "type": "text",
      "value": "\u003ch2 class\u003d\"presto-h2 wp-block-heading\"\u003eConservative support\u003c/h2\u003e"
    }, {
      "type": "text",
      "value": "\u003cp\u003eVotes for Madigan and Bogdansky signal continued community support for their \u0026#34;parents\u0026#39; rights\u0026#34; views and their blurring of the line traditionally separating municipal and educational agencies. Both are frequent attendees and commenters at meetings of the Ramapo Indian Hills Regional High School\u0026#39;s Board of Education, where Bogdansky\u0026#39;s husband Tom is a Wyckoff member and Madigan previously served 14 years as trustee.  \u003c/p\u003e"
    }, {
      "type": "text",
      "value": "\u003cp\u003eMadigan was at the May 30 board meeting to praise the trustees for their unanimous vote on the newly hired Superintendent Ronnie Tarchichi. He has declared himself a \u0026#34;believer in family values\u0026#34; in this year\u0026#39;s election. Bogdansky is a founder of \u003ca href\u003d\"https://wsnj.org/\"\u003eWomen United for a Stronger New Jersey\u003c/a\u003e encouraging \u0026#34;moderate Republican and Independent women\u0026#34; to run for office,  and sponsoring a mix of Republican and nonpartisan board candidates together at meet-and-greets during election season.\u003c/p\u003e"
    }, {
      "type": "text",
      "value": "\u003cp\u003eLane, a resident since 1991, previously served on the New Milford Borough Council from 1980 to 1982 and 1985 to 1986 and as mayor from 1987 to 1989. He served on the Ramapo Indian Hills Board of Education from 1999 to 2002. He has most recently served on the township\u0026#39;s Environmental Committee.\u003c/p\u003e"
    }],
    "mobile": [{
      "type": "asset",
      "value": "73970952007"
    }, {
      "type": "text",
      "value": "\u003cp\u003eWYCKOFF — In a rare departure from party-line politics, voters bypassed the official GOP nominees to elect three alternate \u0026#34;Republicans for Wyckoff\u0026#34; committee candidates in Tuesday\u0026#39;s primary to run for seats on the Township Committee in November.   \u003c/p\u003e"
