body:
{
  "body": {
    "desktop": [{
      "type": "asset",
      "value": "8420875001"
    }, {
      "type": "text",
      "value": "\u003cp\u003eIf you go into a HomeGoods or HomeSense in New Jersey, don’t be surprised to see store workers wearing something more typically associated with a police officer: body cameras. \u003c/p\u003e"
    }, {
      "type": "text",
      "value": "\u003cp\u003eThe owner of the retail stores said that \u003ca href\u003d\"https://www.usatoday.com/story/news/nation/2023/05/23/department-homeland-security-body-worn-camera-policies-officers-agencies/70248665007/\"\u003ebody cameras\u003c/a\u003e will be worn to deter thieves.\u003c/p\u003e"
    }, {
      "type": "text",
      "value": "\u003cp\u003eA spokesperson at TJX — which owns HomeGoods, HomeSense, Marshalls, Sierra and TJ Maxx — said in a statement to NorthJersey.com affiliate USA TODAY that the body cameras have been used in various stores over the past year.\u003c/p\u003e"
    }, {
      "type": "text",
      "value": "\u003cp\u003eNot every store employee will have a body camera, the spokesperson said, but they will be worn by loss prevention associates trained to use them. \u003c/p\u003e"
    }, {
      "type": "asset",
      "value": "73809452007"
    }, {
      "type": "text",
      "value": "\u003cp\u003eThe retail stores hope that the body cameras will add another level of security, reduce crime and prevent escalated incidents from occurring.\u003c/p\u003e"
    }, {
      "type": "text",
      "value": "\u003cp\u003e“Adding cameras is understandable inasmuch as it helps protect staff against abuse and allows them to record any unlawful behavior that they come across,” said Neil Saunders, a retail analyst and managing director of GlobalData. \u003c/p\u003e"
    }, {
      "type": "text",
      "value": "\u003cp\u003e“Sadly, theft and other criminality has been on the rise in retail, so this is a response to that,” Saunders said. \u003c/p\u003e"
    }, {
      "type": "text",
      "value": "\u003ch2 class\u003d\"presto-h2 exclude-from-newsgate wp-block-heading\"\u003eTarget uses \u0026#39;True Scan\u0026#39; to fight retail theft\u003c/h2\u003e"
    }, {
      "type": "text",
      "value": "\u003cp\u003eThe push for more security comes as department stores like Target started adding measures to stop retail theft. For example, the department store\u0026#39;s new “True Scan” will detect if barcodes aren’t scanned while a customer is in a self-checkout lane.\u003c/p\u003e"
    }, {
      "type": "asset",
      "value": "11465619002"
    }, {
      "type": "text",
      "value": "\u003cp\u003eTarget said it was \u003ca href\u003d\"https://www.usatoday.com/story/money/2023/09/26/target-store-closures-blame-retail-theft/70973950007/\"\u003eclosing nine stores\u003c/a\u003e in New York City, the San Francisco Bay area, Portland and Seattle, explicitly citing retail theft as the driving factor for the closures.\u003c/p\u003e"
    }, {
      "type": "text",
      "value": "\u003cp\u003eBut the National Retail Federation, a trade group, previously pulled back a statistic it promoted saying that organized retail crime accounted for nearly half of retail inventory loss — the number was in fact much smaller. \u003c/p\u003e"
    }, {
      "type": "text",
      "value": "\u003cp\u003eThat came after a November investigation by trade publication \u003ca href\u003d\"https://www.retaildive.com/news/retailers-crime-problem-numbers/699107/\"\u003eRetail Dive\u003c/a\u003e, which found discrepancies in the NRF’s data. \u003c/p\u003e"
    }, {
      "type": "text",
      "value": "\u003ch2 class\u003d\"presto-h2 wp-block-heading\"\u003eWill it work?\u003c/h2\u003e"
    }, {
      "type": "text",
      "value": "\u003cp\u003e“I think the jury’s still out on this,” said Brian Higgins, a former chief of the Bergen County police.\u003c/p\u003e"
    }, {
      "type": "text",
      "value": "\u003cp\u003eAs with police officers, it will help in the case of any store employee accused of misconduct, said Higgins, an adjunct professor at John Jay College of Criminal Justice in Manhattan.\u003c/p\u003e"
    }, {
      "type": "asset",
      "value": "6007699002"
    }, {
      "type": "text",
      "value": "\u003cp\u003e“It’s a combination of protecting their employees — making sure their employees actually are appropriate,” Higgins said. But the policy would also be another option for employers whose rules do not allow security to go after or physically touch someone suspected of shoplifting, said Higgins. \u003c/p\u003e"
    }, {
      "type": "text",
      "value": "\u003cp\u003eAccording to the National Retail Federation, \u003ca href\u003d\"https://cdn.nrf.com/sites/default/files/2023-09/NRF_National_Retail_Security_Survey_2023.pdf\"\u003e11% of retailers\u003c/a\u003e surveyed said they were piloting or testing the use of body cameras in their stores. \u003c/p\u003e"
    }, {
      "type": "text",
      "value": "\u003ch2 class\u003d\"presto-h2 wp-block-heading\"\u003e\u0026#39;Knee-jerk reaction\u0026#39; \u003c/h2\u003e"
    }, {
      "type": "text",
      "value": "\u003cp\u003eJason Williams,  associate professor of justice studies at Montclair State University, decried the move as “a desperate, knee-jerk reaction.” \u003c/p\u003e"
    }, {
      "type": "asset",
      "value": "3166925001"
    }, {
      "type": "text",
      "value": "\u003cp\u003e“Putting their retail workers on the front lines of fighting retail theft opens up a pandora\u0026#39;s box of risks and liability,” he said in an email. \u003c/p\u003e"
    }, {
      "type": "text",
      "value": "\u003cp\u003e“I also wonder what impact this may have on racial minorities who already face increased surveillance in retail shopping spaces based on negative racial-ethnic stereotypes,” he said.\u003c/p\u003e"
    }, {
      "type": "text",
      "value": "\u003cp\u003e\u003cem\u003eThis article contains information from USA TODAY.\u003c/em\u003e\u003c/p\u003e"
    }, {
      "type": "text",
      "value": "\u003cp\u003e\u003cem\u003eDaniel Munoz covers business, consumer affairs, labor and the economy for NorthJersey.com and The Record. \u003c/em\u003e\u003c/p\u003e"
    }, {
      "type": "text",
      "value": "\u003cp\u003e\u003cem\u003eEmail: \u003c/em\u003e\u003ca href\u003d\"mailto:munozd@northjersey.com\" target\u003d\"_blank\" rel\u003d\"noreferrer noopener\"\u003e\u003cem\u003emunozd@northjersey.com\u003c/em\u003e\u003c/a\u003e\u003cem\u003e; Twitter:\u003c/em\u003e\u003ca href\u003d\"https://twitter.com/DanielMunoz100\" target\u003d\"_blank\" rel\u003d\"noreferrer noopener\"\u003e\u003cem\u003e@danielmunoz100\u003c/em\u003e\u003c/a\u003e \u003cem\u003eand \u003ca href\u003d\"https://www.facebook.com/people/Daniel-Munoz-Journalist/61559022552294/\"\u003eFacebook\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/em\u003e\u003c/p\u003e"
    }],
    "mobile": [{
      "type": "asset",
      "value": "8420875001"
    }, {
      "type": "text",
      "value": "\u003cp\u003eIf you go into a HomeGoods or HomeSense in New Jersey, don’t be surprised to see store workers wearing something more typically associated with a police officer: body cameras. \u003c/p\u003e"
    }, {
      "type": "text",
      "value": "\u003cp\u003eThe owner of the retail stores said that \u003ca href\u003d\"https://www.usatoday.com/story/news/nation/2023/05/23/department-homeland-security-body-worn-camera-policies-officers-agencies/70248665007/\"\u003ebody cameras\u003c/a\u003e will be worn to deter thieves.\u003c/p\u003e"
    }, {
      "type": "text",
      "value": "\u003cp\u003eA spokesperson at TJX — which owns HomeGoods, HomeSense, Marshalls, Sierra and TJ Maxx — said in a statement to NorthJersey.com affiliate USA TODAY that the body cameras have been used in various stores over the past year.\u003c/p\u003e"
    }, {
      "type": "text",
      "value": "\u003cp\u003eNot every store employee will have a body camera, the spokesperson said, but they will be worn by loss prevention associates trained to use them. \u003c/p\u003e"
    }, {
      "type": "asset",
      "value": "73809452007"
    }, {
      "type": "text",
      "value": "\u003cp\u003eThe retail stores hope that the body cameras will add another level of security, reduce crime and prevent escalated incidents from occurring.\u003c/p\u003e"
    }, {
      "type": "text",
      "value": "\u003cp\u003e“Adding cameras is understandable inasmuch as it helps protect staff against abuse and allows them to record any unlawful behavior that they come across,” said Neil Saunders, a retail analyst and managing director of GlobalData. \u003c/p\u003e"
    }, {
      "type": "text",
      "value": "\u003cp\u003e“Sadly, theft and other criminality has been on the rise in retail, so this is a response to that,” Saunders said. \u003c/p\u003e"
    }, {
      "type": "text",
      "value": "\u003ch2 class\u003d\"presto-h2 exclude-from-newsgate wp-block-heading\"\u003eTarget uses \u0026#39;True Scan\u0026#39; to fight retail theft\u003c/h2\u003e"
    }, {
      "type": "text",
      "value": "\u003cp\u003eThe push for more security comes as department stores like Target started adding measures to stop retail theft. For example, the department store\u0026#39;s new “True Scan” will detect if barcodes aren’t scanned while a customer is in a self-checkout lane.\u003c/p\u003e"
    }, {
      "type": "asset",
      "value": "11465619002"
    }, {
      "type": "text",
      "value": "\u003cp\u003eTarget said it was \u003ca href\u003d\"https://www.usatoday.com/story/money/2023/09/26/target-store-closures-blame-retail-theft/70973950007/\"\u003eclosing nine stores\u003c/a\u003e in New York City, the San Francisco Bay area, Portland and Seattle, explicitly citing retail theft as the driving factor for the closures.\u003c/p\u003e"
    }, {
      "type": "text",
      "value": "\u003cp\u003eBut the National Retail Federation, a trade group, previously pulled back a statistic it promoted saying that organized retail crime accounted for nearly half of retail inventory loss — the number was in fact much smaller. \u003c/p\u003e"
    }, {
      "type": "text",
      "value": "\u003cp\u003eThat came after a November investigation by trade publication \u003ca href\u003d\"https://www.retaildive.com/news/retailers-crime-problem-numbers/699107/\"\u003eRetail Dive\u003c/a\u003e, which found discrepancies in the NRF’s data. \u003c/p\u003e"
    }, {
      "type": "text",
      "value": "\u003ch2 class\u003d\"presto-h2 wp-block-heading\"\u003eWill it work?\u003c/h2\u003e"
    }, {
      "type": "text",
      "value": "\u003cp\u003e“I think the jury’s still out on this,” said Brian Higgins, a former chief of the Bergen County police.\u003c/p\u003e"
    }, {
      "type": "text",
      "value": "\u003cp\u003eAs with police officers, it will help in the case of any store employee accused of misconduct, said Higgins, an adjunct professor at John Jay College of Criminal Justice in Manhattan.\u003c/p\u003e"
    }, {
      "type": "asset",
      "value": "6007699002"
    }, {
      "type": "text",
      "value": "\u003cp\u003e“It’s a combination of protecting their employees — making sure their employees actually are appropriate,” Higgins said. But the policy would also be another option for employers whose rules do not allow security to go after or physically touch someone suspected of shoplifting, said Higgins. \u003c/p\u003e"
    }, {
      "type": "text",
      "value": "\u003cp\u003eAccording to the National Retail Federation, \u003ca href\u003d\"https://cdn.nrf.com/sites/default/files/2023-09/NRF_National_Retail_Security_Survey_2023.pdf\"\u003e11% of retailers\u003c/a\u003e surveyed said they were piloting or testing the use of body cameras in their stores. \u003c/p\u003e"
    }, {
      "type": "text",
      "value": "\u003ch2 class\u003d\"presto-h2 wp-block-heading\"\u003e\u0026#39;Knee-jerk reaction\u0026#39; \u003c/h2\u003e"
    }, {
      "type": "text",
      "value": "\u003cp\u003eJason Williams,  associate professor of justice studies at Montclair State University, decried the move as “a desperate, knee-jerk reaction.” \u003c/p\u003e"
    }, {
      "type": "asset",
      "value": "3166925001"
    }, {
      "type": "text",
      "value": "\u003cp\u003e“Putting their retail workers on the front lines of fighting retail theft opens up a pandora\u0026#39;s box of risks and liability,” he said in an email. \u003c/p\u003e"
    }, {
      "type": "text",
      "value": "\u003cp\u003e“I also wonder what impact this may have on racial minorities who already face increased surveillance in retail shopping spaces based on negative racial-ethnic stereotypes,” he said.\u003c/p\u003e"
    }, {
      "type": "text",
      "value": "\u003cp\u003e\u003cem\u003eThis article contains information from USA TODAY.\u003c/em\u003e\u003c/p\u003e"
    }, {
      "type": "text",
      "value": "\u003cp\u003e\u003cem\u003eDaniel Munoz covers business, consumer affairs, labor and the economy for NorthJersey.com and The Record. \u003c/em\u003e\u003c/p\u003e"
    }, {
      "type": "text",
      "value": "\u003cp\u003e\u003cem\u003eEmail: \u003c/em\u003e\u003ca href\u003d\"mailto:munozd@northjersey.com\" target\u003d\"_blank\" rel\u003d\"noreferrer noopener\"\u003e\u003cem\u003emunozd@northjersey.com\u003c/em\u003e\u003c/a\u003e\u003cem\u003e; Twitter:\u003c/em\u003e\u003ca href\u003d\"https://twitter.com/DanielMunoz100\" target\u003d\"_blank\" rel\u003d\"noreferrer noopener\"\u003e\u003cem\u003e@danielmunoz100\u003c/em\u003e\u003c/a\u003e \u003cem\u003eand \u003ca href\u003d\"https://www.facebook.com/people/Daniel-Munoz-Journalist/61559022552294/\"\u003eFacebook\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/em\u003e\u003c/p\u003e"
    }]
  }
}

