body:
{
  "body": {
    "desktop": [{
      "type": "asset",
      "value": "12084996002"
    }, {
      "type": "text",
      "value": "\u003cp\u003eDENVILLE – All Julia Fox was thinking was to keep it simple.\u003c/p\u003e"
    }, {
      "type": "text",
      "value": "\u003cp\u003eWith leadoff batter and teammate Taylor Heinemann standing on third base with one out in the first inning, the Morris Knolls sophomore was focused on hitting a shot deep enough to the outfield to help her team in the biggest game of the season.\u003c/p\u003e"
    }, {
      "type": "text",
      "value": "\u003cp\u003eFacing a 2-1 hitter’s count, Fox’s sacrifice fly to right field plated Heinemann in what turned out to be the only run of the game to lift Morris Knolls to a 1-0 victory over rival Morris Hills on Saturday in the NJSIAA North 1, Group 3 final. It’s the Golden Eagles second straight sectional title.\u003c/p\u003e"
    }, {
      "type": "text",
      "value": "\u003cp\u003eSenior pitcher Leah Kleczkowski spun a three-hit, complete game shutout with six strikeouts to lead the Denville school to its sixth straight win.\u003c/p\u003e"
    }, {
      "type": "text",
      "value": "\u003cp\u003eMorris Knolls will host Randolph in a Group 3 semifinal on Tuesday. Randolph defeated North Hunterdon, 8-3 on Saturday in the North 2, Group 3 final.\u003c/p\u003e"
    }, {
      "type": "text",
      "value": "\u003cp\u003e“I was looking for a sacrifice fly. I just knew that I didn’t want to pop up in the infield because I knew Taylor and that run could be really crucial,” Fox said. “It’s a great feeling to win again. The whole team plays really well together. It’s not too often you get to experience something like this.”\u003c/p\u003e"
    }, {
      "type": "text",
      "value": "\u003cp\u003eHeinemann came out swinging in the bottom half of the first inning and reached second base when she laced a fly ball to left field on a 2-1 count that tailed away and ended up scored an error. She then stole third just ahead of Fox’s plate appearance.\u003c/p\u003e"
    }, {
      "type": "text",
      "value": "\u003cp\u003e“I just made sure that I got back to the base in time and run as fast to home and make sure I was safe,” Heinemann said. “We’ve put in a lot of work this season and it’s nice to win it again.”\u003c/p\u003e"
    }, {
      "type": "text",
      "value": "\u003cp\u003eThe Golden Eagles could only string together two hits - singles from Makayla Rady in the third and Hailey Poskonka in the fifth - but tight defense and a big-game effort by Kleczkowski was enough to hold it down.\u003c/p\u003e"
    }, {
      "type": "text",
      "value": "\u003cp\u003eMorris Hills (15-13) threatened in the sixth inning when its first two batters reached base, giving the Scarlet Knights runners on first and second with no outs. But the Golden Eagles defense buckled down and Kleczkowski induced the next two batters to pop up in the infield and a ground out.\u003c/p\u003e"
    }, {
      "type": "text",
      "value": "\u003cp\u003e“I’m not surprised it was only a one-run game. I wish it was bigger, but I knew it was going to be a tight game. They [Morris Hills] always put up a good fight. I’m glad we won,” Kleczkowski  said. “Winning by a run is still a win. We’ve prepared all season for this moment. It’s a great feeling knowing the hard work is paying off.”\u003c/p\u003e"
    }, {
      "type": "text",
      "value": "\u003cp\u003eMorris Hills pitcher Zoe Turner was impressive, going six innings on two hits and no runs.\u003c/p\u003e"
    }, {
      "type": "text",
      "value": "\u003ch2 class\u003d\"presto-h2 wp-block-heading\"\u003eWhat it means\u003c/h2\u003e"
    }, {
      "type": "text",
      "value": "\u003cp\u003eMorris Knolls (23-4) is state sectional champions for the second straight year. They’ve reached a sectional final three straight times.\u003c/p\u003e"
    }, {
      "type": "text",
      "value": "\u003cp\u003eIn their four postseason games, the Golden Eagles have only surrendered one run. Morris Knolls has outscored its opponents, 21-1 with three shutouts.,\u003c/p\u003e"
    }, {
      "type": "text",
      "value": "\u003cp\u003eThe Golden Eagles face county and division rival Randolph (20-8) next in a Group 3 semifinal on Tuesday. Morris Knolls defeated the Rams twice during the regular season and have outscored them, 14-3.\u003c/p\u003e"
    }, {
      "type": "text",
      "value": "\u003cp\u003e“This year we’re just a little more experienced compared to last year. We’re just a year older,” Morris Knolls coach Keith Heinemann said. “Leah has been fantastic this whole tournament. Their [Morris Hills] pitching did a great job and kept us off the bases. We’re still alive.”\u003c/p\u003e"
    }, {
      "type": "text",
      "value": "\u003ch2 class\u003d\"presto-h2 wp-block-heading\"\u003eThey said it\u003c/h2\u003e"
    }, {
      "type": "text",
      "value": "\u003cp\u003e“Whenever we play [Morris] Hills, no matter the sport, it’s always an intense game. Our whole motto this season is ‘nothing is given, everything is earned.’ We knew we had to come out, compete and do our jobs. We knew it was going to be a battle. I felt prepared and confident because I have a very strong defense with good hitters behind me. It’s my senior year and you always want to end on a good note. I knew I could do it and I’m happy coach believed in me.” - Kleczkowski\u003c/p\u003e"
    }, {
      "type": "text",
      "value": "\u003cp\u003e“We knew today was going to be different than the last time we played them. Their coaches do a great job and they have some really good young talent. Their pitcher has been lights out all tournament, so we expected the challenge.” - Keith Heinemann\u003c/p\u003e"
    }],
