    }, {
      "type": "text",
      "value": "\u003cp\u003eForeign-born workers made up double-digit percentages of the workforce across North Jersey. \u003c/p\u003e"
    }, {
      "type": "text",
      "value": "\u003cul\u003e\n\u003cli\u003eBergen County: 38.2%\u003c/li\u003e\n\n\n\n\u003cli\u003eEssex County: 35.9%\u003c/li\u003e\n\n\n\n\u003cli\u003eMorris County: 21.6%\u003c/li\u003e\n\n\n\n\u003cli\u003ePassaic County: 39.2%\u003c/li\u003e\n\n\n\n\u003cli\u003eSussex County: 10.9%\u003c/li\u003e\n\u003c/ul\u003e"
    }, {
      "type": "text",
      "value": "\u003cp\u003eNurses were the most common occupation for foreign-born workers in Bergen County. In Essex County, immigrants most typically were construction laborers.\u003c/p\u003e"
    }, {
      "type": "text",
      "value": "\u003cp\u003eIn Morris County, immigrants were most typically managers, while in Passaic County they were production workers and in Sussex County they were most typically secondary school teachers. \u003c/p\u003e"
    }, {
      "type": "text",
      "value": "\u003cp\u003eThe study relied on data from the 2022 American Community Survey, which is compiled by the U.S Census Bureau. \u003c/p\u003e"
    }, {
      "type": "text",
      "value": "\u003cp\u003e\u003cem\u003eThis article contains information from USA TODAY.\u003c/em\u003e\u003c/p\u003e"
    }, {
      "type": "text",
      "value": "\u003cp\u003e\u003cem\u003eDaniel Munoz covers business, consumer affairs, labor and the economy for NorthJersey.com and The Record. \u003c/em\u003e\u003c/p\u003e"
    }, {
      "type": "text",
      "value": "\u003cp\u003e\u003cem\u003eEmail: \u003c/em\u003e\u003ca href\u003d\"mailto:munozd@northjersey.com\" target\u003d\"_blank\" rel\u003d\"noreferrer noopener\"\u003e\u003cem\u003emunozd@northjersey.com\u003c/em\u003e\u003c/a\u003e\u003cem\u003e; Twitter:\u003c/em\u003e\u003ca href\u003d\"https://twitter.com/DanielMunoz100\" target\u003d\"_blank\" rel\u003d\"noreferrer noopener\"\u003e\u003cem\u003e@danielmunoz100\u003c/em\u003e\u003c/a\u003e \u003cem\u003eand \u003ca href\u003d\"https://www.facebook.com/people/Daniel-Munoz-Journalist/61559022552294/\"\u003eFacebook\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/em\u003e\u003c/p\u003e"
    }]
  }
}

contentBody:
{
  "contentBody": [{
    "type": "asset",
    "value": "73971474007"
  }, {
    "type": "text",
    "value": "\u003cp\u003eImmigrant workers participate in New Jersey’s labor force at a higher rate than native-born workers, according to a new report from Stockton University. \u003c/p\u003e"
  }, {
    "type": "text",
    "value": "\u003cp\u003eThe labor participation rate among foreign-born workers was 69.9%, compared to the 64.7% rate by native-born workers in New Jersey, \u003ca href\u003d\"https://stockton.edu/news/2024/data-immigrant-workers.html\"\u003esays the study\u003c/a\u003e from the William J. Hughes Center for Public Policy at Stockton University, in Galloway Township near Atlantic City. \u003c/p\u003e"
  }, {
    "type": "text",
    "value": "\u003cp\u003eIn New Jersey, foreign workers account for 30% of the New Jersey workforce, compared to 18.1% of the national workforce, the study states. \u003c/p\u003e"
  }, {
    "type": "text",
    "value": "\u003cp class\u003d\"exclude-from-newsgate\"\u003e\u003cstrong\u003e\u003cem\u003eStory continues below photo gallery\u003c/em\u003e\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/p\u003e"
  }, {
    "type": "asset",
    "value": "11778576002"
  }, {
    "type": "text",
    "value": "\u003cp\u003e“Contrary to the negative perceptions around immigration and its impact on the economy, actual labor market trends suggest it’s a boon,” said one of the study’s authors, Stockton economist Ramya Devan.\u003c/p\u003e"
  }, {
    "type": "text",
    "value": "\u003cp\u003e“The data shows that immigrant workers are vital to the New Jersey workforce.”\u003c/p\u003e"
  }, {
    "type": "text",
    "value": "\u003ch2 class\u003d\"presto-h2 wp-block-heading\"\u003eWhat\u0026#39;s a labor participation rate? \u003c/h2\u003e"
  }, {
    "type": "text",
    "value": "\u003cp\u003eAccording to the \u003ca href\u003d\"https://fred.stlouisfed.org/series/CIVPART\"\u003eFederal Reserve Bank of St. Louis\u003c/a\u003e, the labor participation rate is the \u0026#34;percentage of the population that is either working or actively looking for work.\u0026#34; \u003c/p\u003e"
  }, {
    "type": "asset",
    "value": "73332387007"
  }, {
    "type": "text",
    "value": "\u003cp\u003eIn April, \u003ca href\u003d\"https://www.northjersey.com/story/news/2024/04/16/immigrants-have-large-wage-earning-tax-paying-impact-in-nj/73330661007/\"\u003ea separate report said\u003c/a\u003e that every thousand immigrants entering the New Jersey labor market earn a combined $21 million in wages in the first year and pay $1.8 million in state and local taxes.  \u003c/p\u003e"
  }, {
    "type": "text",
    "value": "\u003cp\u003eAnd in five years, that same group of people will likely earn a combined $33 million in wages and contribute $2.9 million in state and local taxes. \u003c/p\u003e"
  }, {
    "type": "text",
    "value": "\u003cp\u003eThat report was prepared jointly by New Jersey Policy Perspective, a progressive think tank, and the New York City-based Immigration Research Initiative, a nonpartisan think tank. \u003c/p\u003e"
  }, {
    "type": "text",
    "value": "\u003cp\u003eBoth studies come as immigration more broadly becomes a hot-button issue going into the 2024 presidential election. \u003c/p\u003e"
  }, {
    "type": "text",
    "value": "\u003cp\u003eBoth former President Donald Trump, a Republican, and President Joe Biden, a Democrat, \u003ca href\u003d\"https://www.northjersey.com/story/news/politics/elections/2024/02/29/biden-trump-visit-border-cities/72774535007/\"\u003evisited the southern border\u003c/a\u003e with Mexico over the winter to tout their immigration agendas. \u003c/p\u003e"
  }, {
    "type": "text",
    "value": "\u003cp\u003eTrump, for example, claimed that many migrants are dangerous and “coming from prisons,” despite research that shows the \u003ca href\u003d\"https://www.northjersey.com/story/news/politics/elections/2024/01/21/texas-governor-reshapes-border-immigration/72166240007/\"\u003econtrary to be true\u003c/a\u003e. \u003c/p\u003e"
  }, {
    "type": "text",
    "value": "\u003cp\u003eEarlier this month, Biden \u003ca href\u003d\"https://www.usatoday.com/videos/news/politics/2024/06/04/president-biden-targets-border-crossings-asylum-in-executive-order/73971474007/\"\u003esigned an executive order\u003c/a\u003e that will cap entry and prevent migrants from seeking asylum if they cross into the U.S. illegally — a move which \u003ca href\u003d\"https://www.northjersey.com/story/news/2024/06/07/biden-asylum-nj-unaccompanied-minors-crossing-border/73999999007/\"\u003esparked intense backlash\u003c/a\u003e from immigrant rights organizations, including several across New Jersey. \u003c/p\u003e"
  }, {
    "type": "text",
    "value": "\u003cp\u003eThe move “effectively closes the border to thousands of immigrant families seeking asylum,” said Raquel Morsy, a member of the progressive activist group Make the Road New Jersey. \u003c/p\u003e"
  }, {
    "type": "text",
    "value": "\u003cp\u003e“Most Americans support a fair and legal asylum system that does not penalize vulnerable families seeking protection at our southern border,” Morsy continued. \u003c/p\u003e"
  }, {
    "type": "text",
    "value": "\u003ch2 class\u003d\"presto-h2 wp-block-heading\"\u003eStockton\u0026#39;s study by the numbers \u003c/h2\u003e"
  }, {
    "type": "text",
    "value": "\u003cp\u003eThe Stockton study found that a large portion of foreign-born workers were software developers, especially in Hudson, Hunterdon and Middlesex counties. Software development was the second-most common occupation in Bergen and Somerset counties among immigrant workers, the study stated. \u003c/p\u003e"
  }, {
    "type": "text",
    "value": "\u003cp\u003eIn those five counties, there were about 35,000 foreign-born software developers. Additionally, across New Jersey, there were more than 26,000 foreign-born nurses and nursing assistants, according to the study. \u003c/p\u003e"
  }, {
    "type": "text",
    "value": "\u003ch2 class\u003d\"presto-h2 wp-block-heading\"\u003eCounty breakdown \u003c/h2\u003e"
  }, {
    "type": "text",
    "value": "\u003cp\u003eForeign-born workers made up double-digit percentages of the workforce across North Jersey. \u003c/p\u003e"
  }, {
    "type": "text",
    "value": "\u003cul\u003e\n\u003cli\u003eBergen County: 38.2%\u003c/li\u003e\n\n\n\n\u003cli\u003eEssex County: 35.9%\u003c/li\u003e\n\n\n\n\u003cli\u003eMorris County: 21.6%\u003c/li\u003e\n\n\n\n\u003cli\u003ePassaic County: 39.2%\u003c/li\u003e\n\n\n\n\u003cli\u003eSussex County: 10.9%\u003c/li\u003e\n\u003c/ul\u003e"
  }, {
    "type": "text",
    "value": "\u003cp\u003eNurses were the most common occupation for foreign-born workers in Bergen County. In Essex County, immigrants most typically were construction laborers.\u003c/p\u003e"
  }, {
    "type": "text",
    "value": "\u003cp\u003eIn Morris County, immigrants were most typically managers, while in Passaic County they were production workers and in Sussex County they were most typically secondary school teachers. \u003c/p\u003e"
  }, {
    "type": "text",
    "value": "\u003cp\u003eThe study relied on data from the 2022 American Community Survey, which is compiled by the U.S Census Bureau. \u003c/p\u003e"
  }, {
    "type": "text",
    "value": "\u003cp\u003e\u003cem\u003eThis article contains information from USA TODAY.\u003c/em\u003e\u003c/p\u003e"
  }, {
    "type": "text",
    "value": "\u003cp\u003e\u003cem\u003eDaniel Munoz covers business, consumer affairs, labor and the economy for NorthJersey.com and The Record. \u003c/em\u003e\u003c/p\u003e"
  }, {
    "type": "text",
    "value": "\u003cp\u003e\u003cem\u003eEmail: \u003c/em\u003e\u003ca href\u003d\"mailto:munozd@northjersey.com\" target\u003d\"_blank\" rel\u003d\"noreferrer noopener\"\u003e\u003cem\u003emunozd@northjersey.com\u003c/em\u003e\u003c/a\u003e\u003cem\u003e; Twitter:\u003c/em\u003e\u003ca href\u003d\"https://twitter.com/DanielMunoz100\" target\u003d\"_blank\" rel\u003d\"noreferrer noopener\"\u003e\u003cem\u003e@danielmunoz100\u003c/em\u003e\u003c/a\u003e \u003cem\u003eand \u003ca href\u003d\"https://www.facebook.com/people/Daniel-Munoz-Journalist/61559022552294/\"\u003eFacebook\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/em\u003e\u003c/p\u003e"
  }]
}

body:
{
  "body": {
    "desktop": [{
      "type": "asset",
      "value": "5668984002"
    }, {
      "type": "text",
      "value": "\u003cp\u003eNew York Waterway has announced that children can ride free on all ferry routes between New Jersey and Manhattan this summer, starting July 5 and running through Labor Day, Sept. 2. Under this promotion, two children under the age of 12 can ride free when accompanied by one paying adult.\u003c/p\u003e"
    }, {
      "type": "text",
      "value": "\u003cp\u003eThe ferry service offers a family-friendly, economical, and traffic-free way to travel to New York City, providing opportunities to enjoy the views and attractions of New York Harbor from open-air top decks. NY Waterway also offers free, multi-route shuttles from the Midtown terminal to popular destinations throughout the city.\u003c/p\u003e"
    }, {
      "type": "text",
      "value": "\u003cp\u003eSeveral New Jersey ferry terminals provide low-cost parking, with free parking available at South Amboy and Port Liberte in Jersey City. Additionally, many NY Waterway ferry terminals are accessible via the Hudson-Bergen Light Rail Line, NJ TRANSIT (rail and bus), and free NY Waterway buses.\u003c/p\u003e"
    }, {
      "type": "asset",
      "value": "3417809001"
    }, {
      "type": "text",
      "value": "\u003cp\u003eFor more details on the Kids Ride Free promotion, visit \u003ca href\u003d\"http://nywaterway.com/KidsFree\" data-type\u003d\"link\" data-id\u003d\"nywaterway.com/KidsFree\"\u003enywaterway.com/KidsFree\u003c/a\u003e.\u003c/p\u003e"
    }, {
      "type": "text",
      "value": "\u003cp\u003eNY Waterway encourages ferry riders to download their App 4.0, available on the App Store and Google Play, to easily access information, locate shuttles, and purchase, store, and activate ferry tickets.\u003c/p\u003e"
    }],
    "mobile": [{
      "type": "asset",
      "value": "5668984002"
    }, {
      "type": "text",
      "value": "\u003cp\u003eNew York Waterway has announced that children can ride free on all ferry routes between New Jersey and Manhattan this summer, starting July 5 and running through Labor Day, Sept. 2. Under this promotion, two children under the age of 12 can ride free when accompanied by one paying adult.\u003c/p\u003e"
    }, {
      "type": "text",
      "value": "\u003cp\u003eThe ferry service offers a family-friendly, economical, and traffic-free way to travel to New York City, providing opportunities to enjoy the views and attractions of New York Harbor from open-air top decks. NY Waterway also offers free, multi-route shuttles from the Midtown terminal to popular destinations throughout the city.\u003c/p\u003e"
    }, {
